WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.451
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:15.180
MODERATOR: The
Gardner lecture given

00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:19.110
by the Department of Aeronautics
and Astronautics at MIT.

00:00:19.110 --> 00:00:23.340
Lester Gardner was a
friend of the department,

00:00:23.340 --> 00:00:25.830
was interested
throughout his career

00:00:25.830 --> 00:00:28.770
in aeronautical
engineering, and was

00:00:28.770 --> 00:00:32.100
instrumental in creating
the Hunsaker professorship.

00:00:32.100 --> 00:00:36.150
He was mostly a publicist of the
field of aerospace engineering

00:00:36.150 --> 00:00:40.170
as it's now called,
and was the publisher

00:00:40.170 --> 00:00:44.940
for more than 10 years of
aviation and aeronautical

00:00:44.940 --> 00:00:49.770
engineering, and was
quite a noted contributor

00:00:49.770 --> 00:00:52.650
in his own right to
aeronautical engineering

00:00:52.650 --> 00:00:54.720
in the middle of the century.

00:00:54.720 --> 00:00:57.510
Today, for only the second time
in the history of the lecture,

00:00:57.510 --> 00:01:00.450
we have two lecturers
who will jointly,

00:01:00.450 --> 00:01:05.850
and in a somewhat coordinated
fashion, give the lecture.

00:01:05.850 --> 00:01:10.530
This past week was
the 10th anniversary

00:01:10.530 --> 00:01:13.620
of the flight of the
Daedalus airplane,

00:01:13.620 --> 00:01:18.030
human powered airplane,
from Crete to Santorini,

00:01:18.030 --> 00:01:22.040
perhaps recreating the
mythical flight of Daedalus.

00:01:22.040 --> 00:01:24.990
Are you going to talk
about the myth, John?

00:01:24.990 --> 00:01:25.740
You are.

00:01:25.740 --> 00:01:31.320
I won't take any more
of John's ammunition.

00:01:31.320 --> 00:01:33.540
And we thought it was
an appropriate time

00:01:33.540 --> 00:01:38.520
to commemorate the entire
history of human powered flight

00:01:38.520 --> 00:01:43.740
and asked two of the principals
to come and be with us

00:01:43.740 --> 00:01:46.380
today to share their
view of this history

00:01:46.380 --> 00:01:50.210
from the insider's view.

00:01:50.210 --> 00:01:53.690
I can recall, literally
as a young man,

00:01:53.690 --> 00:01:56.180
that we had at MIT a
series of I believe

00:01:56.180 --> 00:02:01.200
it was three
symposia on low speed

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:05.850
and motorless flight in the
late '60s and early '70s,

00:02:05.850 --> 00:02:09.780
largely organized by
the late Jim Nash Weber,

00:02:09.780 --> 00:02:12.300
and brought together
at that time

00:02:12.300 --> 00:02:15.960
some of the leading
figures and builders

00:02:15.960 --> 00:02:18.960
of human powered airplanes
up to that point.

00:02:18.960 --> 00:02:21.480
And my recollection of
them was the last of them

00:02:21.480 --> 00:02:24.942
was in about 1974.

00:02:24.942 --> 00:02:28.320
Does anyone remember if that's--

00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:29.270
'76.

00:02:29.270 --> 00:02:32.270
And that was still before
any of the Kremer prizes

00:02:32.270 --> 00:02:36.275
had actually been won if
my recollection serves me.

00:02:36.275 --> 00:02:37.560
Is that right?

00:02:37.560 --> 00:02:38.990
Yeah.

00:02:38.990 --> 00:02:42.470
So we decided to make this,
in some sense, a joint event,

00:02:42.470 --> 00:02:45.170
that there was
today are organized

00:02:45.170 --> 00:02:48.250
by Mark Drela and
Carolyn [? Thotusky, ?]

00:02:48.250 --> 00:02:51.800
the technical symposium
on low speed and motorless

00:02:51.800 --> 00:02:56.120
flight, to bring together
some of the ongoing technical

00:02:56.120 --> 00:02:59.090
efforts in that area and
then followed by the lecture

00:02:59.090 --> 00:03:00.440
this afternoon.

00:03:00.440 --> 00:03:03.040
Well, let me introduce very
briefly our two lecturers.

00:03:03.040 --> 00:03:08.870
THey're, interestingly,
both founders and chairmen

00:03:08.870 --> 00:03:15.230
of various small to medium
size flight vehicle companies

00:03:15.230 --> 00:03:17.750
I guess you would say it.

00:03:17.750 --> 00:03:22.040
Paul Macready is a figure
who needs little introduction

00:03:22.040 --> 00:03:25.410
in the world of modern
aerospace engineering.

00:03:25.410 --> 00:03:30.960
He's the founder and chairman
of AeroVironment, Incorporated,

00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:33.950
a company that
builds specialized

00:03:33.950 --> 00:03:35.720
aircraft with a
particular emphasis

00:03:35.720 --> 00:03:38.990
on advanced electric
power systems.

00:03:38.990 --> 00:03:40.550
He's a member of
the National Academy

00:03:40.550 --> 00:03:43.790
and the American Academy
of Arts and Sciences.

00:03:43.790 --> 00:03:47.600
And as you know, and probably
will hear more about,

00:03:47.600 --> 00:03:51.800
is the father of
the Gossamer family

00:03:51.800 --> 00:03:57.900
of lightweight human
powered vehicles.

00:03:57.900 --> 00:04:01.820
Jon Langford is the founder,
president, and chairman

00:04:01.820 --> 00:04:05.570
of Aurora Flight Sciences,
a company which specializes

00:04:05.570 --> 00:04:10.250
in flight vehicles specialized
to the high altitude

00:04:10.250 --> 00:04:14.060
environment, and
including the Perseus

00:04:14.060 --> 00:04:16.070
proof of concept vehicle.

00:04:16.070 --> 00:04:19.100
He was the program manager
for the Daedalus project

00:04:19.100 --> 00:04:23.450
and the spark plug for several
of the other previous human

00:04:23.450 --> 00:04:27.170
powered airplanes at MIT.

00:04:27.170 --> 00:04:29.990
And in the interim
between two of those,

00:04:29.990 --> 00:04:33.230
left MIT where he
received his degrees

00:04:33.230 --> 00:04:36.890
to go off and work on what
became the F-117 stealth

00:04:36.890 --> 00:04:39.440
fighter at Lockheed
Martin, or at that time

00:04:39.440 --> 00:04:40.970
the Lockheed Corporation.

00:04:40.970 --> 00:04:44.060
So without further
time delay, I'll

00:04:44.060 --> 00:04:46.370
introduce Paul MacCready
who, as I understand it,

00:04:46.370 --> 00:04:48.966
will speak first.

00:04:48.966 --> 00:04:52.354
[APPLAUSE]

00:04:57.690 --> 00:04:59.770
MACCREADY: Thank you
and good afternoon,

00:04:59.770 --> 00:05:03.600
friends of airplanes, human
powered airplanes, challenges,

00:05:03.600 --> 00:05:06.070
and fun.

00:05:06.070 --> 00:05:09.510
This is a field that's been
going on for a long time.

00:05:09.510 --> 00:05:13.050
There's been a huge
amount written about it.

00:05:13.050 --> 00:05:15.480
In the short time we
have this afternoon,

00:05:15.480 --> 00:05:20.350
there's no possible way of
going over this huge history.

00:05:20.350 --> 00:05:25.350
So one is referenced to a
lot of books that exist.

00:05:25.350 --> 00:05:28.005
I've written up a
version of my talk,

00:05:28.005 --> 00:05:30.600
a version that doesn't have much
in the way of illustrations.

00:05:30.600 --> 00:05:32.685
I'm going to show lots of
slides and videos here.

00:05:32.685 --> 00:05:33.810
So it'll be very different.

00:05:33.810 --> 00:05:35.730
But the written
version does have

00:05:35.730 --> 00:05:37.500
a list of a lot of
the publications

00:05:37.500 --> 00:05:41.010
that you can read that
really give all the details

00:05:41.010 --> 00:05:43.710
on the facts of what
happened in this field, how

00:05:43.710 --> 00:05:49.500
the field began, and events
that have taken place

00:05:49.500 --> 00:05:51.420
until fairly recently.

00:05:51.420 --> 00:05:55.470
Also, I will show towards
the end, a video doing more

00:05:55.470 --> 00:06:00.600
with much less that shows
Gossamer Condor and Gossamer

00:06:00.600 --> 00:06:03.420
Albatross very briefly,
which you'll be hearing

00:06:03.420 --> 00:06:05.200
a lot about in the talk.

00:06:05.200 --> 00:06:08.400
But also it shows some of
the things to which those

00:06:08.400 --> 00:06:11.790
have led fitting into a theme of
the doing more with much less.

00:06:11.790 --> 00:06:15.150
And we give copies of that
video to anybody who wants them.

00:06:15.150 --> 00:06:17.700
But I only have a couple
of loose ones here.

00:06:17.700 --> 00:06:20.870
But if any of you will
sign your name over there

00:06:20.870 --> 00:06:22.620
or put a card in
the little bowls

00:06:22.620 --> 00:06:27.120
on the far side of that
table, apparently MIT

00:06:27.120 --> 00:06:29.460
has some way of
magically seeing to it

00:06:29.460 --> 00:06:32.160
that you will receive
one, because we'll just

00:06:32.160 --> 00:06:35.080
send them a bunch.

00:06:35.080 --> 00:06:41.030
I wanted to not duplicate
what's in the written literature

00:06:41.030 --> 00:06:43.580
but try and look a little
broader perspectives.

00:06:43.580 --> 00:06:47.810
And the broadest perspective
is start way back looking

00:06:47.810 --> 00:06:52.640
at what flight has meant
to creatures on earth.

00:06:52.640 --> 00:06:55.250
Several hundred
million years ago,

00:06:55.250 --> 00:07:00.500
it became obvious in
evolutionary survival

00:07:00.500 --> 00:07:04.760
challenges that the air
was a wonderful avenue

00:07:04.760 --> 00:07:09.545
to get up into away from the
things that would eat you.

00:07:09.545 --> 00:07:14.060
And instead of having to crawl
over slimy things on the ground

00:07:14.060 --> 00:07:18.170
and fall rocks and so on,
there is a nice, pure air

00:07:18.170 --> 00:07:23.810
to let you escape things,
travel long distances,

00:07:23.810 --> 00:07:26.280
look for prey, et cetera.

00:07:26.280 --> 00:07:29.840
And there's such
value in getting up

00:07:29.840 --> 00:07:32.960
into this added
dimension in spite

00:07:32.960 --> 00:07:35.150
of all the difficulties
associated with it where

00:07:35.150 --> 00:07:38.960
you have to have wings, and
need light wing loadings.

00:07:38.960 --> 00:07:44.360
And you're fragile and you have
to have a good bit of energy

00:07:44.360 --> 00:07:45.960
and a lot of specialization.

00:07:45.960 --> 00:07:48.380
But the features are such
that I think something

00:07:48.380 --> 00:07:51.380
like 3/4 of insects fly now.

00:07:51.380 --> 00:07:56.180
And obviously, other
natural flyers--

00:07:56.180 --> 00:08:01.040
birds, and bats, and pterosaurs
were great success stories.

00:08:01.040 --> 00:08:05.000
And some of them still are.

00:08:05.000 --> 00:08:08.720
To go through this
very quickly, I

00:08:08.720 --> 00:08:15.230
want to start with
the first slide, which

00:08:15.230 --> 00:08:18.110
shows the four arms--

00:08:18.110 --> 00:08:25.930
one human, three belonging to
other vertebrate creatures.

00:08:25.930 --> 00:08:27.640
And the three top ones fly.

00:08:27.640 --> 00:08:30.880
And because this
ecological niche of flight

00:08:30.880 --> 00:08:35.799
requires the things that grow
from little creatures' DNA

00:08:35.799 --> 00:08:37.720
requires either
they flap something.

00:08:37.720 --> 00:08:39.220
They're flapping their arms.

00:08:39.220 --> 00:08:42.520
And this slide shows
that all of them have,

00:08:42.520 --> 00:08:45.570
including humans, have the
same basic arm structure.

00:08:45.570 --> 00:08:50.490
So they started in the flight
mode at different times

00:08:50.490 --> 00:08:52.460
so they accomplished
it different ways.

00:08:52.460 --> 00:08:55.220
But if you follow all four
of these back far enough,

00:08:55.220 --> 00:08:58.150
you come to certainly
a common ancestor.

00:08:58.150 --> 00:09:00.760
The top pterosaurs
is very simple.

00:09:00.760 --> 00:09:03.820
And it's almost like
a big hang glider.

00:09:03.820 --> 00:09:08.050
And the outer half
of the wing spar

00:09:08.050 --> 00:09:11.270
is just the little finger
that extended way out.

00:09:11.270 --> 00:09:12.700
And birds do it differently.

00:09:12.700 --> 00:09:17.040
Bats use their fingers
as the control surfaces.

00:09:17.040 --> 00:09:22.920
There are a lot of different
insects, some old dragonflies

00:09:22.920 --> 00:09:25.900
I think were about
2 and 1/2 foot

00:09:25.900 --> 00:09:27.460
wingspan back in the old days.

00:09:27.460 --> 00:09:30.040
And even now, they are
wonderful successes,

00:09:30.040 --> 00:09:32.770
nature's helicopters.

00:09:32.770 --> 00:09:35.830
Birds are similar.

00:09:35.830 --> 00:09:39.070
Hummingbirds are similar,
nature's helicopters.

00:09:39.070 --> 00:09:43.330
And the hummingbird looks very
similar to the hawk moth, which

00:09:43.330 --> 00:09:45.020
occupies the same flight niche.

00:09:45.020 --> 00:09:46.840
They look the same
but one's an insect.

00:09:46.840 --> 00:09:50.170
One's a bird.

00:09:50.170 --> 00:09:55.890
The flyers that we're
really in awe of

00:09:55.890 --> 00:09:58.320
are some of the more
efficient birds.

00:09:58.320 --> 00:09:59.430
Here's an albatross.

00:09:59.430 --> 00:10:02.250
Just magnificent for going
out over long distances

00:10:02.250 --> 00:10:03.030
over the ocean.

00:10:03.030 --> 00:10:05.970
And you'll notice
very high wingspan.

00:10:05.970 --> 00:10:07.470
Looks almost like a sail plane.

00:10:07.470 --> 00:10:09.440
And essentially no tail.

00:10:09.440 --> 00:10:11.970
It can whip out a tail
for some tight maneuvers,

00:10:11.970 --> 00:10:14.880
but most of the time,
it does not use one.

00:10:14.880 --> 00:10:16.860
Same is true of the
frigate bird, which

00:10:16.860 --> 00:10:22.710
soars on very gentle up currents
and convection over oceans,

00:10:22.710 --> 00:10:25.230
because it's using
these weak thermals that

00:10:25.230 --> 00:10:27.150
are about a meter a second.

00:10:27.150 --> 00:10:31.710
It ends up as a superb low speed
soarer with low sinking speed.

00:10:31.710 --> 00:10:34.050
The albatross using
dynamic scoring

00:10:34.050 --> 00:10:36.540
is more like a
bullet with wings.

00:10:36.540 --> 00:10:38.280
Goes a lot faster.

00:10:38.280 --> 00:10:39.870
Each one fitting their niche.

00:10:39.870 --> 00:10:41.890
And then when you
get over land, there

00:10:41.890 --> 00:10:44.380
are many other kinds
of soaring creatures.

00:10:44.380 --> 00:10:47.580
And you start seeing ones that
are very good at the low speed

00:10:47.580 --> 00:10:51.510
flight, maneuverability,
all those feathers doing

00:10:51.510 --> 00:10:53.700
interesting and, we
are beginning to learn,

00:10:53.700 --> 00:11:00.220
very efficient things in order
to make flight work very well.

00:11:00.220 --> 00:11:02.370
And we just had a
talk today on the way

00:11:02.370 --> 00:11:03.960
wings should join the body.

00:11:03.960 --> 00:11:09.090
It turns out that resulted
from a lot of advanced theory.

00:11:09.090 --> 00:11:11.430
And it turns out they look
just like the vultures that

00:11:11.430 --> 00:11:14.140
have been around for
100 million years.

00:11:14.140 --> 00:11:16.290
So we're learning.

00:11:16.290 --> 00:11:20.880
We now can see bugs,
birds, and bats fly.

00:11:20.880 --> 00:11:23.340
But we can't see
the pterodactyls

00:11:23.340 --> 00:11:26.280
fly because they went extinct
when the dinosaurs went extinct

00:11:26.280 --> 00:11:27.990
65 million years ago.

00:11:27.990 --> 00:11:32.350
As they started,
and the pterosaurs,

00:11:32.350 --> 00:11:34.740
they were rhamphorhynchoids
with tails

00:11:34.740 --> 00:11:37.560
like you see here, actually
rather small creatures,

00:11:37.560 --> 00:11:41.820
more sparrowlike than the
awesome size that you see.

00:11:41.820 --> 00:11:45.420
And then as they got
larger, as time went on,

00:11:45.420 --> 00:11:49.470
the larger ones began
having no tails.

00:11:49.470 --> 00:11:53.760
It turned out an ounce of brains
used an active control seemed

00:11:53.760 --> 00:11:56.640
to be much better than
adding an extra pound of tail

00:11:56.640 --> 00:11:58.620
on the creature.

00:11:58.620 --> 00:12:05.600
So modern ones just flew
like ordinary creatures.

00:12:05.600 --> 00:12:08.950
And this is a
static display model

00:12:08.950 --> 00:12:11.575
of the largest creature
that ever flew,

00:12:11.575 --> 00:12:19.980
the QN pterodactyl, of
which we made a flying model

00:12:19.980 --> 00:12:23.160
to be in a movie called
On The Wing showing

00:12:23.160 --> 00:12:24.910
the evolution of natural
flight, evolution

00:12:24.910 --> 00:12:26.740
of technological flight.

00:12:26.740 --> 00:12:28.900
And there were no
pterodactyls around to take

00:12:28.900 --> 00:12:32.110
pictures of so we made a
replica, only an 18 foot one

00:12:32.110 --> 00:12:34.300
rather than 36 foot
one, because we only

00:12:34.300 --> 00:12:37.060
were able to get up through
the temperamental overweight

00:12:37.060 --> 00:12:40.400
adolescent size by the
time the project was done.

00:12:40.400 --> 00:12:41.360
But it worked.

00:12:41.360 --> 00:12:45.280
And in the flights, it
crashed often but never

00:12:45.280 --> 00:12:47.350
when the IMAX cameras
were pointed at it.

00:12:47.350 --> 00:12:50.420
Somehow it knew what
its mission was.

00:12:50.420 --> 00:12:54.140
But to have this thing
fly is just amazing.

00:12:54.140 --> 00:12:55.900
It has no tail.

00:12:55.900 --> 00:12:57.460
Trying to keep it
pointing forwards

00:12:57.460 --> 00:13:00.400
like trying to shoot narrow
with the feathered end forward.

00:13:00.400 --> 00:13:04.400
And it makes you
appreciate nature.

00:13:04.400 --> 00:13:06.370
So wonderful on active control.

00:13:06.370 --> 00:13:08.740
And those other birds
that I showed you

00:13:08.740 --> 00:13:10.810
aren't using the
tail very often.

00:13:10.810 --> 00:13:13.210
It uses active control.

00:13:13.210 --> 00:13:15.020
Humans are edging toward that.

00:13:15.020 --> 00:13:19.140
But most of our planes
have regular tails.

00:13:19.140 --> 00:13:22.470
I've got a video that
will show this flying

00:13:22.470 --> 00:13:23.710
if you start with number one.

00:13:27.670 --> 00:13:29.660
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:13:29.660 --> 00:13:37.102
- On January 15 this year,
in [INAUDIBLE],, California,

00:13:37.102 --> 00:13:40.350
the modern day pterodactyl
flew for the cameras

00:13:40.350 --> 00:13:46.300
in the Smithsonian's
widescreen film On the Wing.

00:13:46.300 --> 00:13:50.170
A part of history we had not
witnessed more than 65 million

00:13:50.170 --> 00:13:52.874
years ago was brought to life.

00:13:52.874 --> 00:13:54.350
Can you imagine?

00:14:01.730 --> 00:14:04.320
The most important
thing on this is

00:14:04.320 --> 00:14:08.610
that it serves as a symbol and
catalyst against people's minds

00:14:08.610 --> 00:14:09.930
[INAUDIBLE].

00:14:09.930 --> 00:14:13.060
What it's done for us
is ensure [INAUDIBLE]

00:14:13.060 --> 00:14:18.140
appreciate the wonderful job
mother nature does as a design.

00:14:18.140 --> 00:14:19.860
Man will not
[INAUDIBLE] something

00:14:19.860 --> 00:14:24.900
this large can be propelled by
biological power, by muscles.

00:14:24.900 --> 00:14:27.280
But nature shows that it was.

00:14:27.280 --> 00:14:29.590
But it's the same
fact [INAUDIBLE]

00:14:29.590 --> 00:14:32.908
wanted across the planet
and realized we didn't

00:14:32.908 --> 00:14:34.200
discover [INAUDIBLE] geography.

00:14:34.200 --> 00:14:36.310
[INAUDIBLE] design an airplane.

00:14:36.310 --> 00:14:41.624
It just changed the
world, because it

00:14:41.624 --> 00:14:46.610
got people's [INAUDIBLE],, I
hope this will be a little bit

00:14:46.610 --> 00:14:47.602
the same way.

00:14:51.070 --> 00:14:51.570
Okay.

00:14:54.560 --> 00:14:56.690
MACCREADY: Nature has
a lot of different ways

00:14:56.690 --> 00:15:02.360
of having flying things, showing
you the four real fliers.

00:15:02.360 --> 00:15:05.780
But then seeds are made so they
have pretty good aerodynamics.

00:15:05.780 --> 00:15:08.150
Pollen is distributed
through the air.

00:15:08.150 --> 00:15:13.550
Spiders on spiderwebs can be
transported hundreds of miles.

00:15:13.550 --> 00:15:16.250
And there are many
different kinds of flight.

00:15:16.250 --> 00:15:17.780
And there are flying fish.

00:15:17.780 --> 00:15:20.072
And many of you won't believe
it but there are actually

00:15:20.072 --> 00:15:20.690
flying squid.

00:15:20.690 --> 00:15:23.540
And they fly in groups
and fly very beautifully.

00:15:23.540 --> 00:15:24.320
Not very far.

00:15:28.310 --> 00:15:37.850
All this is to emphasize
that all energy of creatures

00:15:37.850 --> 00:15:44.700
on earth really comes from
sun with a few exceptions

00:15:44.700 --> 00:15:48.870
and with different
time constants.

00:15:48.870 --> 00:15:54.080
So solar power up here,
you get instantly.

00:15:54.080 --> 00:15:56.900
Fossil fuel is stored
energy of sunlight,

00:15:56.900 --> 00:16:00.090
maybe from many
millions of years ago.

00:16:00.090 --> 00:16:06.050
Up here using wind and heat to
make thermals and sail planes

00:16:06.050 --> 00:16:07.130
fly.

00:16:07.130 --> 00:16:12.530
But also there's
food for muscles,

00:16:12.530 --> 00:16:16.680
which is the energy that
creatures use to get around.

00:16:16.680 --> 00:16:20.870
And so a hawk will
start with this energy.

00:16:20.870 --> 00:16:23.240
And then we'll use
a lot that comes

00:16:23.240 --> 00:16:29.640
from wind and the heating of
the ground making thermals.

00:16:29.640 --> 00:16:32.350
So I've shown a
spectrum of flying

00:16:32.350 --> 00:16:34.480
things that go from
practically no weight up

00:16:34.480 --> 00:16:37.020
to that giant pterosaur.

00:16:37.020 --> 00:16:42.170
And the pterosaur probably
weighed, or pterodactyl QN

00:16:42.170 --> 00:16:46.450
and probably weighed 180 pounds
at about a 36 foot wingspan.

00:16:46.450 --> 00:16:49.330
It was the only one of that
species, the large one,

00:16:49.330 --> 00:16:53.320
found and I doubt that
one found the biggest.

00:16:53.320 --> 00:16:55.000
And you look at
some scaling laws.

00:16:55.000 --> 00:16:57.208
And there's no reason why
they couldn't have had one,

00:16:57.208 --> 00:16:59.770
say, 40 foot in size.

00:16:59.770 --> 00:17:01.510
And there are some
giant versions

00:17:01.510 --> 00:17:05.859
of that hawk or
that vulture that I

00:17:05.859 --> 00:17:09.760
showed that probably
got up to 180 pounds

00:17:09.760 --> 00:17:12.400
and flew about 6
million years ago.

00:17:12.400 --> 00:17:16.000
These began edging towards
people type weight.

00:17:16.000 --> 00:17:18.730
And the giant
vulture was probably

00:17:18.730 --> 00:17:20.410
around 20 foot
wingspan, getting up

00:17:20.410 --> 00:17:25.930
towards span that
involves real airplanes.

00:17:25.930 --> 00:17:28.329
So there got to be,
at the beginning

00:17:28.329 --> 00:17:31.787
of this century, some interest
in human powered flight.

00:17:31.787 --> 00:17:34.120
A lot more interest at the
very beginning because people

00:17:34.120 --> 00:17:35.650
didn't know about engines.

00:17:35.650 --> 00:17:38.100
But once engines
began, the interest

00:17:38.100 --> 00:17:40.480
in human powered flight
sort of evaporated.

00:17:40.480 --> 00:17:43.750
But an early prize was to see
how far you could go just using

00:17:43.750 --> 00:17:46.780
the kinetic energy
of your momentum

00:17:46.780 --> 00:17:52.450
as you got a bicycle
and significant prize.

00:17:52.450 --> 00:17:56.000
Competitions like this do
help the field move along.

00:17:56.000 --> 00:17:59.320
There's another prize put up
in the middle of the '30s.

00:17:59.320 --> 00:18:03.120
This is one of the planes
that attempted to do it.

00:18:03.120 --> 00:18:05.560
And they're really very
great pioneering airplanes.

00:18:05.560 --> 00:18:06.820
Person in there pedaling.

00:18:06.820 --> 00:18:09.580
Couldn't qu9ite climb, but
could greatly extend the glide.

00:18:09.580 --> 00:18:13.570
But I think it then sandbagged
with the requirement

00:18:13.570 --> 00:18:15.610
of the government,
I believe in Italy,

00:18:15.610 --> 00:18:20.380
that this had to
meet all the strength

00:18:20.380 --> 00:18:24.820
requirements of regular
commercial aircraft

00:18:24.820 --> 00:18:27.290
even though it would never
fly higher than 10 feet.

00:18:27.290 --> 00:18:28.610
And that rather limited it.

00:18:28.610 --> 00:18:32.830
It was a great
pioneering effort.

00:18:32.830 --> 00:18:36.830
And then a big
moment in this field,

00:18:36.830 --> 00:18:40.250
there had been a man powered
aircraft group associated

00:18:40.250 --> 00:18:42.950
with the Royal Aeronautical
Society for a while.

00:18:42.950 --> 00:18:45.590
Henry Kremer was a friend
of some of those people.

00:18:45.590 --> 00:18:49.040
And after selling a division
of one of his companies,

00:18:49.040 --> 00:18:54.480
he had a party to celebrate
and asked one of these people

00:18:54.480 --> 00:18:56.720
how is that human powered
airplane stuff going.

00:18:56.720 --> 00:18:58.827
And his friend replied
it wasn't going anyplace.

00:18:58.827 --> 00:19:00.910
They're just sitting around
talking to each other.

00:19:00.910 --> 00:19:02.870
It was going to take
something like a prize

00:19:02.870 --> 00:19:05.750
to make it go to get
that field going.

00:19:05.750 --> 00:19:08.570
And Kremer, who was starting
on his third martini,

00:19:08.570 --> 00:19:10.490
said I'll put up a prize.

00:19:10.490 --> 00:19:13.010
And that was the deep
insight that went

00:19:13.010 --> 00:19:14.407
into what I think is one of--

00:19:14.407 --> 00:19:16.490
you'll find from the
consequence-- one of the most

00:19:16.490 --> 00:19:21.350
creative activities
in the 20th century

00:19:21.350 --> 00:19:26.270
and does get you to think
about the importance of ethanol

00:19:26.270 --> 00:19:29.660
as an energy producer
or liberator.

00:19:29.660 --> 00:19:34.700
Anyhow, Kremer is short
fellow right there.

00:19:34.700 --> 00:19:38.450
And right after his prize
came out, teams in England

00:19:38.450 --> 00:19:41.180
began focusing their attention
on really getting stuff out.

00:19:41.180 --> 00:19:42.740
Not sure which plane this is.

00:19:42.740 --> 00:19:44.090
It might be the Puffin.

00:19:44.090 --> 00:19:49.370
But they built several large
span quite sophisticated,

00:19:49.370 --> 00:19:51.350
though sophisticated--
one of the definitions

00:19:51.350 --> 00:19:53.600
of the dictionary is
unnecessarily complicated.

00:19:53.600 --> 00:19:55.490
You wonder why so
many ribs and so on.

00:19:55.490 --> 00:20:00.960
Well, it's easy to be
judgmental long after the fact.

00:20:00.960 --> 00:20:03.288
This was a great
pioneering activity.

00:20:03.288 --> 00:20:05.330
At the time, illustrated
some of the difficulties

00:20:05.330 --> 00:20:07.160
but illustrates some
of the successes.

00:20:07.160 --> 00:20:09.740
And they were able to actually
get the plane like this

00:20:09.740 --> 00:20:11.810
off into the air
under the high power

00:20:11.810 --> 00:20:15.920
that a pilot can put out for
a very short period of time.

00:20:15.920 --> 00:20:16.878
But it couldn't turn.

00:20:16.878 --> 00:20:18.920
And when it crashed, it
would be six months or so

00:20:18.920 --> 00:20:20.840
before they'd be
back flying again

00:20:20.840 --> 00:20:22.160
because of its complexity.

00:20:26.540 --> 00:20:28.970
Ignore the Albatross
that followed later.

00:20:28.970 --> 00:20:32.960
But there began being various
planes that no need to go into.

00:20:32.960 --> 00:20:34.880
If you really want
to get the history,

00:20:34.880 --> 00:20:38.840
there are bunch of books that
give it in delightful fashion.

00:20:38.840 --> 00:20:40.880
But a bunch of groups
did work on this.

00:20:40.880 --> 00:20:43.100
But the surprising thing
was after the first flush

00:20:43.100 --> 00:20:46.427
of success the
first few years, it

00:20:46.427 --> 00:20:48.260
turned out people weren't
getting any closer

00:20:48.260 --> 00:20:51.720
to winning that prize,
which was ingeniously--

00:20:51.720 --> 00:20:54.290
the rules were ingeniously
put as a figure eight

00:20:54.290 --> 00:20:57.758
flight around two pylons
half a mile apart.

00:20:57.758 --> 00:21:00.050
Required something to be able
to turn left, turn right,

00:21:00.050 --> 00:21:02.467
go up high enough the beginning
and the end of the flight.

00:21:02.467 --> 00:21:05.030
So it couldn't be just some
exaggerated ground effect.

00:21:05.030 --> 00:21:05.612
Machine.

00:21:05.612 --> 00:21:07.070
It took long enough
so you couldn't

00:21:07.070 --> 00:21:09.530
get by on that
anaerobic power, where

00:21:09.530 --> 00:21:12.320
a pilot can put out one and 1/2
horsepower for a few seconds.

00:21:12.320 --> 00:21:15.620
It had to be the aerobic
continuous power where

00:21:15.620 --> 00:21:19.430
he's down to more like a
third or half a horsepower.

00:21:23.470 --> 00:21:27.470
I had no interest in
the subject at all,

00:21:27.470 --> 00:21:30.860
but I had a background that
was fairly useful to prepare me

00:21:30.860 --> 00:21:34.757
for this-- incidentally about
the only interest I was--

00:21:34.757 --> 00:21:36.590
like everybody in the
[INAUDIBLE] activity--

00:21:36.590 --> 00:21:38.520
I was aware of the competition.

00:21:38.520 --> 00:21:42.140
And I think in the 1974
MIT event I was back here

00:21:42.140 --> 00:21:46.970
and actually saw movies of
one of those planes fly--

00:21:46.970 --> 00:21:48.850
I think might have
been Jupiter--

00:21:48.850 --> 00:21:51.680
and it was very dramatic
and that imprinted itself

00:21:51.680 --> 00:21:52.470
on my mind.

00:21:52.470 --> 00:21:56.690
But I had no interest
in the whole topic.

00:21:56.690 --> 00:22:01.130
But I did have a background
in model airplane building

00:22:01.130 --> 00:22:04.700
when I was a youngster and
I'd make these indoor models--

00:22:04.700 --> 00:22:06.540
somebody else's, I'm
not a youngster here.

00:22:06.540 --> 00:22:09.890
But thing like this
would weigh about a gram

00:22:09.890 --> 00:22:13.220
for the model for a one meter
wingspan, and the rubber band

00:22:13.220 --> 00:22:16.190
that powers another gram, and
they've gotten up to one hour

00:22:16.190 --> 00:22:17.800
flight indoors with these.

00:22:17.800 --> 00:22:19.950
The propeller just
slowly goes like that

00:22:19.950 --> 00:22:22.670
and it moves through the
air like a fish gliding

00:22:22.670 --> 00:22:24.380
through an aquarium.

00:22:24.380 --> 00:22:26.330
It's a art form--
turns out to be

00:22:26.330 --> 00:22:29.810
amazingly analogous
structurally and stability

00:22:29.810 --> 00:22:33.740
and control-wise to
Gossamer airplane.

00:22:33.740 --> 00:22:36.670
Is also familiar with hang
gliders because with my sons

00:22:36.670 --> 00:22:39.710
we were playing with these
as the sport is getting

00:22:39.710 --> 00:22:44.450
pioneered in Southern California
in the first half of the 1975--

00:22:44.450 --> 00:22:50.930
1970s and, again, came to
appreciate the virtues of tubes

00:22:50.930 --> 00:22:53.090
in compression,
exterior wire bracing,

00:22:53.090 --> 00:22:56.900
simple sail construction.

00:22:56.900 --> 00:23:00.815
Well-- and I was also involved
in streamlined bike activities.

00:23:00.815 --> 00:23:03.170
The International Human-powered
Vehicle Association

00:23:03.170 --> 00:23:08.930
began getting going, then go as
fast as you can with no rules.

00:23:08.930 --> 00:23:12.650
Rules stifled bike development
for a long time now.

00:23:12.650 --> 00:23:17.180
Just go as fast as you can and
great developments took place.

00:23:17.180 --> 00:23:20.090
And through that I was getting
very familiar with exercise

00:23:20.090 --> 00:23:24.410
physiology, championship
bicyclists, exercise training

00:23:24.410 --> 00:23:26.720
techniques, and so on.

00:23:26.720 --> 00:23:32.620
And then on vacation
trip my mind--

00:23:32.620 --> 00:23:35.170
got away with my
mind daydreaming.

00:23:35.170 --> 00:23:37.490
It's very hard to find any
daydreaming time these days

00:23:37.490 --> 00:23:38.448
with so many pressures.

00:23:38.448 --> 00:23:41.650
But the only time I've ever
gotten a big idea is when I

00:23:41.650 --> 00:23:44.330
been daydreaming--
mind thinks for itself,

00:23:44.330 --> 00:23:46.180
decides on what it
wants to think about,

00:23:46.180 --> 00:23:49.220
and it was connecting
some things in my mind--

00:23:49.220 --> 00:23:51.280
one of which was--

00:23:51.280 --> 00:23:53.440
gotten a debt because,
as a good guy,

00:23:53.440 --> 00:23:57.233
guaranteed somebody's note
at a bank for a $100,000 loan

00:23:57.233 --> 00:23:58.900
to start a company
which didn't succeed.

00:23:58.900 --> 00:23:59.900
He couldn't pay it back.

00:23:59.900 --> 00:24:03.550
I was stuck with a note
and didn't have the cash

00:24:03.550 --> 00:24:04.550
to do anything about it.

00:24:04.550 --> 00:24:06.300
But I assure you that
was stuck in my mind

00:24:06.300 --> 00:24:08.200
some place even
on vacation trips.

00:24:08.200 --> 00:24:10.900
Your unconscious would
nibble away at that.

00:24:10.900 --> 00:24:12.520
I was aware that--

00:24:12.520 --> 00:24:15.360
had no interest in the
Kremer prize which I had--

00:24:15.360 --> 00:24:17.710
I did know was up
to 50,000 pounds.

00:24:17.710 --> 00:24:21.395
But, once, in middle
vacation going--

00:24:21.395 --> 00:24:23.650
I looked the newspaper,
I found the pound was

00:24:23.650 --> 00:24:25.840
worth $2 at that
time, and suddenly

00:24:25.840 --> 00:24:28.790
this light bulb just
glowed over my head.

00:24:28.790 --> 00:24:30.820
Why that's the same
amount as my debt,

00:24:30.820 --> 00:24:34.240
how exciting and important
human-powered flight is.

00:24:34.240 --> 00:24:39.538
So I began concentrating
on it very hard and tried--

00:24:39.538 --> 00:24:42.080
I'm an aerodynamicist, I should
be able to figure things out.

00:24:42.080 --> 00:24:44.200
But every design I
came up with were

00:24:44.200 --> 00:24:48.020
like those people in England
that were not able to--

00:24:48.020 --> 00:24:49.750
that didn't come
close to succeeding

00:24:49.750 --> 00:24:53.533
with big teams, and big,
complex, elegant airplanes,

00:24:53.533 --> 00:24:55.700
and I batted my head against
that wall for a while--

00:24:55.700 --> 00:24:57.220
gave up, went off
did other things.

00:24:57.220 --> 00:24:59.380
I was doing some
studies of bird flight--

00:24:59.380 --> 00:25:02.338
timing how long it takes for a
bird to go around in a circle,

00:25:02.338 --> 00:25:04.630
look at the bank angle, you
can tell it's flight speed,

00:25:04.630 --> 00:25:05.780
flight radius.

00:25:05.780 --> 00:25:08.620
And then I began comparing
this bird with that bird,

00:25:08.620 --> 00:25:12.040
comparing-- doing the scaling
laws that relate different

00:25:12.040 --> 00:25:14.590
size, and weights, and speed,
compared that with a hang

00:25:14.590 --> 00:25:18.880
glider and the sail plane, and
this hobby thing suddenly--

00:25:18.880 --> 00:25:20.440
as often happens--

00:25:20.440 --> 00:25:22.170
I thought back about
human-powered flight

00:25:22.170 --> 00:25:24.010
and realized, hey,
it's all solved.

00:25:24.010 --> 00:25:26.410
There was an easy
way to do it which

00:25:26.410 --> 00:25:29.293
is if you make something large
enough and light enough--

00:25:29.293 --> 00:25:31.960
if the weight just doesn't go up
and you can keep making a thing

00:25:31.960 --> 00:25:34.085
like a hang glider bigger,
and bigger, and bigger--

00:25:34.085 --> 00:25:35.350
no matter how ugly it is--

00:25:35.350 --> 00:25:39.130
if it's big enough, you can
fly it on very low power

00:25:39.130 --> 00:25:41.255
and so immediately made a model.

00:25:41.255 --> 00:25:43.330
As soon as that
vacation trip was done,

00:25:43.330 --> 00:25:49.168
you see here that tested how
you make a very large wing.

00:25:49.168 --> 00:25:50.710
I had no knowledge
of wing structure,

00:25:50.710 --> 00:25:52.918
but I had knowledge of indoor
models and so on-- just

00:25:52.918 --> 00:25:54.170
used logic.

00:25:54.170 --> 00:25:56.690
This is the best way to make
a lightweight structure.

00:25:56.690 --> 00:25:58.750
And that's the model.

00:25:58.750 --> 00:26:02.020
And about two months later we
were flying the first version

00:26:02.020 --> 00:26:03.150
of the Gossamer Condor--

00:26:03.150 --> 00:26:06.340
55-pound weight with
my 105-pound son

00:26:06.340 --> 00:26:10.570
flying at the time, who
would fly as slowly as six

00:26:10.570 --> 00:26:13.317
miles an hour and had a
lot of problems with it--

00:26:13.317 --> 00:26:13.900
in everything.

00:26:13.900 --> 00:26:16.750
It exaggerated the
problems so that you can

00:26:16.750 --> 00:26:18.873
identify and understand them.

00:26:18.873 --> 00:26:20.290
But it had one
great feature which

00:26:20.290 --> 00:26:27.970
is it was quick to repair,
modify, alter, redesign.

00:26:27.970 --> 00:26:31.510
And if it crashed on landing--

00:26:31.510 --> 00:26:34.080
you didn't grab it properly
as it slid a little sideways--

00:26:34.080 --> 00:26:36.930
because stability
control was just awful--

00:26:36.930 --> 00:26:40.060
and a tube broke, you'd
get a broom handle

00:26:40.060 --> 00:26:42.400
and some duct tape and tape
the broom handle back on.

00:26:42.400 --> 00:26:44.062
You'd be flying in five minutes.

00:26:44.062 --> 00:26:46.270
That accident would have
kept those people in England

00:26:46.270 --> 00:26:48.580
not flying for something
like six months.

00:26:48.580 --> 00:26:51.580
So we got huge amount of
flight experience out of this.

00:26:51.580 --> 00:26:54.490
Learned all the difficulties.

00:26:54.490 --> 00:26:57.190
It's just so thin, fragile.

00:26:57.190 --> 00:27:00.370
It seems as though the
shadow looks a little more

00:27:00.370 --> 00:27:02.560
solid than the airplane does.

00:27:02.560 --> 00:27:06.340
Well, we began
sensing the reason

00:27:06.340 --> 00:27:08.650
for some of these problems
was an apparent mass

00:27:08.650 --> 00:27:11.050
effect that exists on all
airplanes but completely

00:27:11.050 --> 00:27:12.230
negligible.

00:27:12.230 --> 00:27:15.040
The mass of air to
which you're coupled

00:27:15.040 --> 00:27:18.190
on the plane and regular
plane is negligible

00:27:18.190 --> 00:27:19.990
compared to the airplane here.

00:27:19.990 --> 00:27:22.090
It was much, much
larger than the mass

00:27:22.090 --> 00:27:23.555
of the airplane and the person.

00:27:23.555 --> 00:27:25.180
And it was affecting
what was going on,

00:27:25.180 --> 00:27:30.580
affecting the controllability,
and the total energy

00:27:30.580 --> 00:27:33.730
during acceleration, during
turning, why we couldn't turn.

00:27:33.730 --> 00:27:38.860
We used advanced computer
models on stability and control,

00:27:38.860 --> 00:27:40.480
trying to get
answers about this.

00:27:40.480 --> 00:27:44.230
And all the computer
models said is those flight

00:27:44.230 --> 00:27:46.930
observations you made-- which is
there was nothing you could do

00:27:46.930 --> 00:27:49.660
to that wing in
drag [INAUDIBLE],,

00:27:49.660 --> 00:27:51.730
drag rotors-- that would
make any difference,

00:27:51.730 --> 00:27:55.098
just keep flying straight and
maybe sliding off on a side.

00:27:55.098 --> 00:27:56.473
And that's what
we saw and that's

00:27:56.473 --> 00:27:58.610
what the computer said,
yeah, that's right.

00:27:58.610 --> 00:28:00.400
It didn't tell us
what to do, so I

00:28:00.400 --> 00:28:04.420
put a couple of sticks together
that had Gossamer Condor

00:28:04.420 --> 00:28:07.180
proportions, shoved it
around in the swimming pool--

00:28:07.180 --> 00:28:08.120
as you see here.

00:28:08.120 --> 00:28:11.380
Here's one of the sons doing
it, with your hand just feeling

00:28:11.380 --> 00:28:14.650
like a force balance
in a wind tunnel.

00:28:14.650 --> 00:28:17.230
But here, you're working the
fluid almost a thousand times

00:28:17.230 --> 00:28:20.260
denser than air so that
apparent mass effect is really

00:28:20.260 --> 00:28:21.460
emphasized.

00:28:21.460 --> 00:28:25.650
And within five minutes,
we had the main answers,

00:28:25.650 --> 00:28:29.660
what the inarticulate computers
hadn't been able to tell us,

00:28:29.660 --> 00:28:32.500
and we came up with
the final design.

00:28:32.500 --> 00:28:34.450
A lot of the designing
in detail was

00:28:34.450 --> 00:28:38.230
done by friendly friends who
I'd lured into the project.

00:28:38.230 --> 00:28:39.820
It was very much a team effort.

00:28:39.820 --> 00:28:40.737
They were experienced.

00:28:40.737 --> 00:28:44.110
They had built
airplanes, boats, so on.

00:28:44.110 --> 00:28:46.580
And this is the way
the drawings were done.

00:28:46.580 --> 00:28:49.390
We never had a set of plans
until after the prize was won.

00:28:49.390 --> 00:28:51.550
The prize was not
for drawing plans.

00:28:51.550 --> 00:28:53.710
It was for doing
the flight, and we

00:28:53.710 --> 00:28:55.660
didn't have to worry
about numbering plans

00:28:55.660 --> 00:28:57.460
and storing them and so on.

00:28:57.460 --> 00:29:00.610
Yeah, we used computers on
prop design and some airfoil

00:29:00.610 --> 00:29:02.830
and stuff like that
when it was needed.

00:29:02.830 --> 00:29:04.930
When this was okay,
that's what we did.

00:29:04.930 --> 00:29:08.710
And wind took care of erasing
stuff when you were done.

00:29:08.710 --> 00:29:10.090
It was appropriate technology.

00:29:10.090 --> 00:29:11.770
It's not the way to
design airliners,

00:29:11.770 --> 00:29:13.540
but that's what we did.

00:29:13.540 --> 00:29:17.200
And a little bit on the
construction-- aluminum tube.

00:29:17.200 --> 00:29:19.630
If the wall was too thick,
you'd chemically mill down

00:29:19.630 --> 00:29:21.790
to the correct thickness.

00:29:21.790 --> 00:29:25.970
And looking in the wing, just
a lot of pieces of tape-covered

00:29:25.970 --> 00:29:26.470
Mylar.

00:29:26.470 --> 00:29:29.440
Now we're making a
double surface plane.

00:29:29.440 --> 00:29:35.380
And got my youngest son--
or middle son, Tyler.

00:29:35.380 --> 00:29:37.570
Another person had been
doing the main test flying,

00:29:37.570 --> 00:29:39.710
but that person had
to go off to Belgium.

00:29:39.710 --> 00:29:41.945
So Tyler did a bunch
of the test flying.

00:29:41.945 --> 00:29:43.570
Next, just working
out on a [INAUDIBLE]

00:29:43.570 --> 00:29:45.160
to build himself up.

00:29:45.160 --> 00:29:47.710
The amount of power you have to
put out is about proportional

00:29:47.710 --> 00:29:49.150
to your weight.

00:29:49.150 --> 00:29:50.410
And a young person--

00:29:50.410 --> 00:29:52.740
older person can do it.

00:29:52.740 --> 00:29:55.090
And he had some flight
skills and he works cheap,

00:29:55.090 --> 00:29:57.010
so we got him.

00:29:57.010 --> 00:30:00.310
And with a new version,
it was flying beautifully

00:30:00.310 --> 00:30:05.980
right off the bat, but still a
lot of little problems with it

00:30:05.980 --> 00:30:09.340
and structural and we needed to
lighten its weight and so on.

00:30:09.340 --> 00:30:13.120
But it was kind of
going in the right way.

00:30:13.120 --> 00:30:16.405
We then decided we--
it was going okay

00:30:16.405 --> 00:30:18.880
and now we needed a
real pilot, Bryan Allen.

00:30:18.880 --> 00:30:23.835
And here he is with some
of his exercise training.

00:30:23.835 --> 00:30:25.210
We were very lucky
to locate him.

00:30:25.210 --> 00:30:28.810
He's a gifted pilot, but
also very good on stamina--

00:30:28.810 --> 00:30:32.530
willing to fit into such a
project, works on airplanes,

00:30:32.530 --> 00:30:35.000
and we were just so lucky.

00:30:35.000 --> 00:30:37.960
But the biggest-- the most
important thing in this project

00:30:37.960 --> 00:30:42.200
was lots of luck, and getting
him was a big part of that.

00:30:42.200 --> 00:30:43.507
Now let's have the next video--

00:30:43.507 --> 00:30:45.840
rather than showing you a lot
of Gossamer Condor things,

00:30:45.840 --> 00:30:50.060
this video, taken from the kiosk
at the National [AUDIO OUT]

00:30:50.060 --> 00:30:51.500
will show some of the flying.

00:30:51.500 --> 00:30:52.167
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:30:52.167 --> 00:30:54.790
- [INAUDIBLE] led by Dr. Paul
MacCready of AeroVironment

00:30:54.790 --> 00:30:57.380
Incorporated.

00:30:57.380 --> 00:31:00.320
The development of the
Gossamer Condor took a year

00:31:00.320 --> 00:31:03.260
and was a friends
and family affair.

00:31:03.260 --> 00:31:05.660
The earliest version
demonstrated the validity

00:31:05.660 --> 00:31:09.800
of the team's design approach--
a huge, feather-weight, slow

00:31:09.800 --> 00:31:12.563
flying aircraft.

00:31:12.563 --> 00:31:14.230
- [INAUDIBLE],, the
air's speed is okay.

00:31:14.230 --> 00:31:15.360
Sock it to it.

00:31:15.360 --> 00:31:17.212
- It's twisted a bit.

00:31:20.620 --> 00:31:23.110
Let's get up about 3 feet.

00:31:23.110 --> 00:31:23.610
Okay.

00:31:27.993 --> 00:31:30.240
[INAUDIBLE] 35.

00:31:30.240 --> 00:31:31.305
- Let's go, [INAUDIBLE]!

00:31:31.305 --> 00:31:32.970
- Stop pedalling!

00:31:32.970 --> 00:31:34.000
Stop pedalling.

00:31:34.000 --> 00:31:35.870
Pull a left!

00:31:35.870 --> 00:31:38.722
- You go get him.

00:31:38.722 --> 00:31:39.222
- Oh!

00:31:43.090 --> 00:31:43.820
The [INAUDIBLE]--

00:31:43.820 --> 00:31:44.320
- Oh, yeah.

00:31:44.320 --> 00:31:45.175
Hold this up.

00:31:45.175 --> 00:31:45.967
- Hold it up there.

00:31:45.967 --> 00:31:46.283
You look--

00:31:46.283 --> 00:31:46.756
- Hold it up there--

00:31:46.756 --> 00:31:47.256
- Get up--

00:31:47.256 --> 00:31:50.070
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:31:50.070 --> 00:31:53.970
- The builders learned from both
crashes and computer programs,

00:31:53.970 --> 00:31:58.080
and they made many changes
to create the final airplane

00:31:58.080 --> 00:32:03.170
with a 96 foot wingspan and
weighing only 70 pounds.

00:32:03.170 --> 00:32:05.280
- Where are the nails?

00:32:05.280 --> 00:32:07.440
- Six minutes and
two turns later,

00:32:07.440 --> 00:32:10.823
the only obstacle left is
the 10-foot finish pole.

00:32:10.823 --> 00:32:14.204
[SHOUTING]

00:32:22.420 --> 00:32:24.800
On his prize-winning
flight, Bryan Allen

00:32:24.800 --> 00:32:27.770
landed with the
words "we did it,"

00:32:27.770 --> 00:32:31.730
a tribute to the teamwork that
made the Gossamer Condor fly

00:32:31.730 --> 00:32:33.115
into aviation history.

00:32:37.150 --> 00:32:38.090
[END PLAYBACK]

00:32:38.090 --> 00:32:41.540
MACCREADY: This is a
shot during that flight.

00:32:41.540 --> 00:32:44.015
The flight was great.

00:32:44.015 --> 00:32:44.890
The pressure was off.

00:32:44.890 --> 00:32:45.830
The prize was won.

00:32:45.830 --> 00:32:47.390
A good bit of it
had been consumed.

00:32:47.390 --> 00:32:52.190
And expenses on the project--

00:32:52.190 --> 00:32:54.530
and there was always a team.

00:32:54.530 --> 00:32:56.510
I'm sorry that the
only way you'll

00:32:56.510 --> 00:32:59.030
get the idea of the teams
on all of these projects

00:32:59.030 --> 00:33:01.010
is to read the publications.

00:33:01.010 --> 00:33:03.110
They were not a
one-person effort at all.

00:33:03.110 --> 00:33:05.600
The team is very important.

00:33:05.600 --> 00:33:10.240
Here's Kremer at the
prize event in England.

00:33:10.240 --> 00:33:11.090
Wonderful for us.

00:33:11.090 --> 00:33:16.550
Prince Charles gave the
actual trophy and check

00:33:16.550 --> 00:33:19.342
from the Royal
Aeronautical Society.

00:33:19.342 --> 00:33:21.300
And setting it up in the
National Air and Space

00:33:21.300 --> 00:33:26.480
Museum, where we have recently,
last year, cleaned it all up.

00:33:26.480 --> 00:33:29.570
And we didn't think was going to
last there more than two years,

00:33:29.570 --> 00:33:33.657
because it was also baggy and
floppy and tape pulling out.

00:33:33.657 --> 00:33:35.240
But now it's kind
of cleaned up and we

00:33:35.240 --> 00:33:37.640
put a mannequin in that
looks just like Bryan Allen

00:33:37.640 --> 00:33:41.090
so people can see
which way it goes.

00:33:41.090 --> 00:33:45.020
And the kiosk video
shows a lot of people

00:33:45.020 --> 00:33:46.670
flying it after
the prize flight--

00:33:46.670 --> 00:33:48.380
old ladies and little
kids and so on--

00:33:48.380 --> 00:33:51.140
to make people
realize this was kind

00:33:51.140 --> 00:33:55.820
of a person-next-door project,
not some big aerospace firm

00:33:55.820 --> 00:33:56.930
activity.

00:33:56.930 --> 00:33:59.120
Kremer came out
with a new prize,

00:33:59.120 --> 00:34:01.437
taking 18 years for
somebody to go a mile.

00:34:01.437 --> 00:34:03.520
He thought it'd take another
18 years for somebody

00:34:03.520 --> 00:34:05.450
to go 23 miles across
the English Channel.

00:34:05.450 --> 00:34:08.630
But we realized if we just
cloned the Gossamer Condor, cut

00:34:08.630 --> 00:34:10.159
down the power required--

00:34:10.159 --> 00:34:13.520
20% or so-- and
selected the right day,

00:34:13.520 --> 00:34:16.590
the pilot could fly much,
much longer and do the job.

00:34:16.590 --> 00:34:17.750
But now we use carbon--

00:34:17.750 --> 00:34:20.662
now, no new ideas
in the aerodynamics.

00:34:20.662 --> 00:34:22.370
It wasn't all that
interesting a project.

00:34:22.370 --> 00:34:29.179
But in the structure, carbon
tubing instead of the aluminum

00:34:29.179 --> 00:34:31.969
and many more ribs,
much more accurately

00:34:31.969 --> 00:34:39.230
contoured airfoil and so
on, and flew perfectly right

00:34:39.230 --> 00:34:39.800
off the bat.

00:34:39.800 --> 00:34:42.380
But like our
previous ones, there

00:34:42.380 --> 00:34:44.570
were various-- there
were some crashes.

00:34:44.570 --> 00:34:47.000
And as we assess
the situation, it

00:34:47.000 --> 00:34:49.927
looked as though to get enough
testing on this, to get it over

00:34:49.927 --> 00:34:52.219
to England and back was going
to cost a bunch of money.

00:34:52.219 --> 00:34:54.590
We were able to get the
DuPont company to spring

00:34:54.590 --> 00:34:56.600
for sponsoring the
project and letting

00:34:56.600 --> 00:34:59.840
us keep the prize money as
a carrot out in front of us.

00:34:59.840 --> 00:35:03.790
And so the project
charged ahead.

00:35:03.790 --> 00:35:08.690
Here doing some quick flights
as we began building--

00:35:08.690 --> 00:35:10.740
cleaning up the first plane.

00:35:10.740 --> 00:35:12.970
We'd put my youngest
son-- because he only

00:35:12.970 --> 00:35:15.470
weighed 80 pounds-- in it, and
you could just push it around

00:35:15.470 --> 00:35:19.610
and nothing would get
hurt if it hit hard.

00:35:19.610 --> 00:35:23.300
And then fix it
up and Bryan Allen

00:35:23.300 --> 00:35:26.480
was doing a good job
flying, but he was only

00:35:26.480 --> 00:35:29.810
able to fly about 20 minutes
with this particular prop.

00:35:29.810 --> 00:35:33.470
And by going to a different prop
that Jean Larrabee had designed

00:35:33.470 --> 00:35:37.760
and people at MIT here helped
us with or actually did

00:35:37.760 --> 00:35:38.780
the detail design.

00:35:38.780 --> 00:35:39.950
Then we did the building.

00:35:39.950 --> 00:35:42.410
This optimized the
prop very elegantly

00:35:42.410 --> 00:35:45.530
for the crew's
condition on the flight.

00:35:45.530 --> 00:35:49.130
And plane-- Bryan did a
flight of about an hour and 20

00:35:49.130 --> 00:35:51.360
minutes, landed and felt he
could've gone on forever.

00:35:51.360 --> 00:35:54.350
So we were off to
England miraculously.

00:35:54.350 --> 00:35:56.000
We didn't know how
to get to England.

00:35:56.000 --> 00:35:58.370
But in the middle of the
night, I get a phone call--

00:35:58.370 --> 00:36:02.060
very muffled of all those
airplanes at Nellis Air Force

00:36:02.060 --> 00:36:05.270
Base at 2:00 AM on Saturday.

00:36:05.270 --> 00:36:06.990
And that was the message.

00:36:06.990 --> 00:36:10.430
And so there was a C-130 from
the Royal Aeronautical Society.

00:36:10.430 --> 00:36:13.040
Three human-powered airplanes
were all crammed inside it,

00:36:13.040 --> 00:36:14.630
and all our
belongings, and so on.

00:36:14.630 --> 00:36:16.970
It was just another--

00:36:16.970 --> 00:36:19.650
good luck is more
important than anything.

00:36:19.650 --> 00:36:23.270
Bryan Allen doing a lot
of training in England,

00:36:23.270 --> 00:36:26.000
and training with
Joe Mastropaolo

00:36:26.000 --> 00:36:30.170
in hot, humid conditions with
no air blowing on him in order

00:36:30.170 --> 00:36:31.610
to get training
for what it would

00:36:31.610 --> 00:36:34.430
be like in the plane
during the flight.

00:36:36.950 --> 00:36:40.580
Then on the day when the
weather looked as though it just

00:36:40.580 --> 00:36:44.300
might be okay, we're on
the Warren near Dover

00:36:44.300 --> 00:36:46.220
getting ready for
the flight to begin.

00:36:46.220 --> 00:36:48.150
Let's have the next.

00:36:48.150 --> 00:36:51.290
Wait a minute, before
the video begins--

00:36:51.290 --> 00:36:56.240
the take off, because of that
prop, was great for cruise

00:36:56.240 --> 00:36:58.220
but it was rather
poor for takeoff

00:36:58.220 --> 00:36:59.780
when you're going very slowly.

00:36:59.780 --> 00:37:01.410
And we hadn't realized how bad.

00:37:01.410 --> 00:37:03.740
We'd always done it on
big airports before.

00:37:03.740 --> 00:37:07.580
Here we put out a little
runway, a piece of plywood.

00:37:07.580 --> 00:37:09.320
The wing running wasn't
done quite right,

00:37:09.320 --> 00:37:12.050
and the plane got
just off the plywood,

00:37:12.050 --> 00:37:17.270
and the little toy 2-ounce wheel
fell into the only little hole

00:37:17.270 --> 00:37:20.430
in that big acre of
concrete, and snapped.

00:37:20.430 --> 00:37:21.830
And so there is the plane.

00:37:21.830 --> 00:37:23.210
That was our start.

00:37:23.210 --> 00:37:25.912
And this is an audience
participation exercise.

00:37:25.912 --> 00:37:27.620
You're asked to see
if you can figure out

00:37:27.620 --> 00:37:32.990
who is the DuPont representative
who is running the project.

00:37:32.990 --> 00:37:36.620
But about 10 minutes
later, the plane

00:37:36.620 --> 00:37:38.960
started on what was the
most fantastic journey,

00:37:38.960 --> 00:37:42.620
most fantastic physical stamina
event that I've ever heard of.

00:37:45.548 --> 00:37:47.500
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:37:47.500 --> 00:37:51.220
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:37:51.220 --> 00:37:52.140
- Doing okay, Bryan.

00:37:56.145 --> 00:37:57.270
You're looking good, Bryan.

00:37:57.270 --> 00:37:59.205
You're looking good.

00:37:59.205 --> 00:38:00.730
Heading in there, good buddy.

00:38:00.730 --> 00:38:03.240
It's downhill from here.

00:38:03.240 --> 00:38:08.200
- Numbly, Bryan pedals on,
no longer counting time.

00:38:08.200 --> 00:38:12.080
His flight instruments no longer
function and his water supply

00:38:12.080 --> 00:38:14.580
is exhausted.

00:38:14.580 --> 00:38:17.690
When at last he sights the
French coast through the haze,

00:38:17.690 --> 00:38:19.250
his heart sinks.

00:38:19.250 --> 00:38:21.350
It is still three miles away.

00:38:25.634 --> 00:38:26.586
- Go, Bryan.

00:38:29.920 --> 00:38:34.400
- For Bryan, each moment
now has become a victory.

00:38:34.400 --> 00:38:38.810
Dehydrated, suffering
severe leg cramps,

00:38:38.810 --> 00:38:41.720
his judgment has begun to waver.

00:38:41.720 --> 00:38:44.300
Briefly, he considers
crashing on the rocky point.

00:38:51.900 --> 00:38:55.100
Instead, he fights to save the
Albatross from the turbulence

00:38:55.100 --> 00:38:57.600
that [INAUDIBLE] added and
nearly wrecks it a few feet

00:38:57.600 --> 00:38:58.100
offshore.

00:39:03.110 --> 00:39:04.096
- Come on, Bryan.

00:39:09.026 --> 00:39:14.449
- Then slowly, at
last, he's done.

00:39:24.802 --> 00:39:28.750
[CHEERING]

00:39:28.750 --> 00:39:31.717
[END PLAYBACK]

00:39:31.717 --> 00:39:33.300
MACCREADY: And a
bigger team, but many

00:39:33.300 --> 00:39:37.005
of the same members from the
first team on this project.

00:39:37.005 --> 00:39:39.075
We never had so much
pressure, and I've never

00:39:39.075 --> 00:39:39.825
been so exhausted.

00:39:39.825 --> 00:39:43.260
And all the people, we
were using psychokinetics

00:39:43.260 --> 00:39:46.890
or telekinesis or
whatever for lifting Bryan

00:39:46.890 --> 00:39:49.620
during that whole
thing, from the boat.

00:39:49.620 --> 00:39:50.420
It was tough.

00:39:54.310 --> 00:39:56.220
This shows at
Aerovironment, we're

00:39:56.220 --> 00:39:57.860
into a lot of different things.

00:39:57.860 --> 00:40:00.550
And when these human-powered
airplanes began,

00:40:00.550 --> 00:40:04.480
it spawned a big list of
projects that are ongoing now.

00:40:04.480 --> 00:40:07.060
And we're growing,
I think 240 people.

00:40:07.060 --> 00:40:10.870
We're growing as fast as we can
find really qualified people.

00:40:10.870 --> 00:40:14.050
And it relates to ground
things and energy things,

00:40:14.050 --> 00:40:17.900
with a lot of commonality
between all these.

00:40:17.900 --> 00:40:22.390
There are many too many things
to describe that the Gossamer

00:40:22.390 --> 00:40:23.500
aircraft led to.

00:40:23.500 --> 00:40:26.530
The important point is that
it led to just so many things

00:40:26.530 --> 00:40:28.090
that I heartily
recommend everybody

00:40:28.090 --> 00:40:31.650
should get $100,000 debt
to get themselves moving.

00:40:31.650 --> 00:40:36.520
And also get some projects
that don't require a product

00:40:36.520 --> 00:40:38.500
the next year,
but let you really

00:40:38.500 --> 00:40:42.960
explore the frontiers
of some technology.

00:40:42.960 --> 00:40:45.660
Here's the first solar flight.

00:40:45.660 --> 00:40:48.868
They're 80-pound sun,
still, because we

00:40:48.868 --> 00:40:50.410
didn't have many
solar cells and that

00:40:50.410 --> 00:40:51.970
actually climbed
a little bit just

00:40:51.970 --> 00:40:55.930
on the power of solar cells.

00:40:55.930 --> 00:41:00.460
This is Bionic Bat, that won
two of the Kremer Speed Prizes.

00:41:00.460 --> 00:41:04.060
And here's a flying
telephone pole

00:41:04.060 --> 00:41:05.740
that's very hard to describe.

00:41:05.740 --> 00:41:11.290
But it's amazing what things
will fly out at El Mirage.

00:41:11.290 --> 00:41:17.980
And well, I'll go back on this.

00:41:17.980 --> 00:41:20.300
As a human power--

00:41:20.300 --> 00:41:23.550
this is the last of
the human power slides.

00:41:23.550 --> 00:41:25.550
This is a human-powered
airplane that our son's

00:41:25.550 --> 00:41:26.630
figured out how to do.

00:41:26.630 --> 00:41:29.640
And this kid can walk
a whole city block

00:41:29.640 --> 00:41:30.890
with his hands in his pockets.

00:41:30.890 --> 00:41:33.680
And just the up-current of
the air going around his body

00:41:33.680 --> 00:41:36.780
and over his forehead will
keep that airplane up there.

00:41:36.780 --> 00:41:40.790
We have not found this to
be a marketable talent,

00:41:40.790 --> 00:41:45.350
unfortunately, and he's just
home goofing off right now.

00:41:45.350 --> 00:41:47.000
Let's have the next videos.

00:41:50.560 --> 00:41:52.530
Made some big planes--

00:41:52.530 --> 00:41:54.290
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:41:54.290 --> 00:41:55.490
But also some little planes.

00:41:55.490 --> 00:41:57.890
Here you see a
little thing weighs

00:41:57.890 --> 00:42:04.490
1.8 grams zooming around the
room, and under radio control.

00:42:04.490 --> 00:42:08.460
It's got a video camera in it,
and all the telemetry, and so

00:42:08.460 --> 00:42:08.960
on.

00:42:12.070 --> 00:42:16.870
Buddy will catch it on this one,
which is the preferred landing

00:42:16.870 --> 00:42:18.850
technique.

00:42:18.850 --> 00:42:23.540
And then on the next
go-around-- we've

00:42:23.540 --> 00:42:27.948
flown these inside
auditoriums like this,

00:42:27.948 --> 00:42:31.448
and now you'll see
what the airplanes saw.

00:42:31.448 --> 00:42:32.490
It was very crude camera.

00:42:32.490 --> 00:42:36.670
We have better ones now,
color, and higher light level,

00:42:36.670 --> 00:42:37.730
and so on.

00:42:37.730 --> 00:42:39.880
But you are there.

00:42:39.880 --> 00:42:42.434
Okay, next video, please.

00:42:42.434 --> 00:42:43.730
[END PLAYBACK]

00:42:43.730 --> 00:42:51.020
The next video, I won't
have time today to show

00:42:51.020 --> 00:42:52.862
you the Birdman contest.

00:42:52.862 --> 00:42:54.320
I'm showing you
things that we did.

00:42:54.320 --> 00:42:55.970
Birdman contest in
Japan, they have

00:42:55.970 --> 00:42:59.060
70 human-power or
glider aircraft

00:42:59.060 --> 00:43:00.580
fly that are just wonderful.

00:43:00.580 --> 00:43:02.070
It's a wonderful training aid.

00:43:02.070 --> 00:43:02.703
It seems silly.

00:43:02.703 --> 00:43:03.620
They're all destroyed.

00:43:03.620 --> 00:43:06.230
They go out from a 10-meter
platform over the water,

00:43:06.230 --> 00:43:09.624
and they all land in the water,
and that's the end of them.

00:43:09.624 --> 00:43:10.555
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

00:43:10.555 --> 00:43:11.180
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:43:11.180 --> 00:43:13.020
But I can show you this--

00:43:13.020 --> 00:43:15.520
"Doing More with Much Less"
that you can get a free copy of.

00:43:23.020 --> 00:43:26.290
- Doing more with less is
a vital feature of a world

00:43:26.290 --> 00:43:29.380
that works, where our
increasing demands are met,

00:43:29.380 --> 00:43:32.260
yet do not overwhelm
the limits of the Earth.

00:43:32.260 --> 00:43:34.330
Some of the unusually
efficient vehicles

00:43:34.330 --> 00:43:36.700
with which Aerovironment
has been involved

00:43:36.700 --> 00:43:39.400
serve as visual
metaphors for the theme.

00:43:39.400 --> 00:43:42.610
Most dramatic is the unmanned,
100-foot, solar-powered

00:43:42.610 --> 00:43:45.550
Pathfinder, here undergoing
low-altitude tests

00:43:45.550 --> 00:43:47.530
in late 1993.

00:43:47.530 --> 00:43:50.770
Versions are aimed at
eternal flight, or at least

00:43:50.770 --> 00:43:53.920
flights for months
above 65,000 feet,

00:43:53.920 --> 00:43:56.020
to carry equipment
for surveillance,

00:43:56.020 --> 00:43:58.477
stratospheric monitoring,
and telecommunications.

00:43:58.477 --> 00:44:01.060
MACCREADY: Last summer, it went
75,000 feet, higher than any--

00:44:01.060 --> 00:44:05.650
- The fragile, 70-pound Gossamer
Albatross was pedaled 23 miles

00:44:05.650 --> 00:44:10.000
across the English Channel in
1979 to win the largest prize

00:44:10.000 --> 00:44:11.080
in aviation history.

00:44:14.260 --> 00:44:17.290
It demonstrates what can
be done with low power

00:44:17.290 --> 00:44:20.920
when new concepts of efficiency
are unleashed by challenges not

00:44:20.920 --> 00:44:23.680
burdened by constraints
from narrow rules

00:44:23.680 --> 00:44:26.260
or the need for
commercial production.

00:44:26.260 --> 00:44:28.810
It is a catalyst
for new perspectives

00:44:28.810 --> 00:44:31.450
that can lead to useful
insights and products

00:44:31.450 --> 00:44:33.160
such as the Pathfinder.

00:44:33.160 --> 00:44:37.720
And incidentally, it was a
tribute to the human spirit.

00:44:37.720 --> 00:44:40.480
The more primitive and
fragile Gossamer Condor

00:44:40.480 --> 00:44:43.582
was the first really successful
human-powered airplane.

00:44:43.582 --> 00:44:46.040
The final version can be seen
at the National Air and Space

00:44:46.040 --> 00:44:50.160
Museum, where it was installed
after this 1977 flight won

00:44:50.160 --> 00:44:51.325
the first Kremer Prize.

00:44:54.360 --> 00:44:56.670
About this time, the
International Human-powered

00:44:56.670 --> 00:44:59.610
Vehicle Association was starting
to organize competitions

00:44:59.610 --> 00:45:03.240
for land vehicles, without the
inhibiting effect of rules--

00:45:03.240 --> 00:45:07.050
just go as fast as you can.

00:45:07.050 --> 00:45:10.860
The streamlined Gold Rush
finally exceeded 65 miles

00:45:10.860 --> 00:45:12.990
per hour, and a
two-person version

00:45:12.990 --> 00:45:17.100
averaged over 50 miles an hour
on a 40-mile freeway trip.

00:45:17.100 --> 00:45:18.870
Later competition
started to include

00:45:18.870 --> 00:45:22.290
some less extreme practical
vehicle categories, no match

00:45:22.290 --> 00:45:25.830
for our wonderful modern cars,
but valuable in suggesting

00:45:25.830 --> 00:45:27.600
that some
transportation need not

00:45:27.600 --> 00:45:30.390
involve large amounts
of steel and oil,

00:45:30.390 --> 00:45:31.870
and can be healthy and fun.

00:45:35.600 --> 00:45:37.820
Pioneering for speed
from human power

00:45:37.820 --> 00:45:40.580
has resulted in some
amazing water vehicles.

00:45:40.580 --> 00:45:43.130
This Flying Fish
hydrofoil lowers drag

00:45:43.130 --> 00:45:45.710
by flying on a submerged wing.

00:45:45.710 --> 00:45:47.930
It has been pedaled
at 18 miles per hour,

00:45:47.930 --> 00:45:50.345
and can beat the best
eight-oared racing shells.

00:45:53.800 --> 00:45:57.280
In 1987, General Motors
teamed with Aerovironment

00:45:57.280 --> 00:46:00.220
for the rapid development
of the GM Sun Racer

00:46:00.220 --> 00:46:02.090
to be entered in the
World Solar Challenge

00:46:02.090 --> 00:46:05.080
to cross Australia on
energy from sunlight.

00:46:05.080 --> 00:46:08.470
It won, speeding 50%
faster than the runner-up,

00:46:08.470 --> 00:46:12.610
and averaging 42 miles per hour
on only a kilowatt of power.

00:46:12.610 --> 00:46:14.590
Batteries to store the
intermittent energy

00:46:14.590 --> 00:46:17.800
from the sun for vehicle use
are essential, and obviously,

00:46:17.800 --> 00:46:19.750
for non-race
purposes, they can be

00:46:19.750 --> 00:46:23.710
charged from the utility grid
without need for solar cells.

00:46:23.710 --> 00:46:26.050
This solar car race
helped emphasize

00:46:26.050 --> 00:46:29.530
that battery-powered
cars can make sense.

00:46:29.530 --> 00:46:32.320
Next, the same team
combined to create

00:46:32.320 --> 00:46:34.990
the battery-powered
Impact demonstrator.

00:46:34.990 --> 00:46:37.000
To show that cars
emitting no pollution

00:46:37.000 --> 00:46:39.220
can be part of our
transportation future,

00:46:39.220 --> 00:46:42.160
it needed adequate range
and snappy acceleration.

00:46:42.160 --> 00:46:44.080
Here, it zooms
from 0 to 60 miles

00:46:44.080 --> 00:46:45.880
per hour in eight seconds.

00:46:45.880 --> 00:46:48.070
Now many groups are
increasing priorities

00:46:48.070 --> 00:46:51.400
on battery-powered vehicles,
vehicle efficiency in general,

00:46:51.400 --> 00:46:54.280
and broader
transportation issues.

00:46:54.280 --> 00:46:57.850
Impractical human-powered
airplanes, streamlined bikes,

00:46:57.850 --> 00:47:01.270
and solar-powered cars now
seem more valuable as some

00:47:01.270 --> 00:47:03.910
of the ideas they ignited
evolve into socially

00:47:03.910 --> 00:47:06.580
desirable vehicles
aimed at a mass market.

00:47:09.440 --> 00:47:11.390
Globally, bikes
are widely employed

00:47:11.390 --> 00:47:15.110
for personal mobility, transport
of goods, and recreation.

00:47:15.110 --> 00:47:18.290
For some uses, battery
assist, a scaled-down version

00:47:18.290 --> 00:47:21.020
of electric car
technology, makes sense.

00:47:21.020 --> 00:47:23.990
You can get the help of a
virtual peddler, here hidden

00:47:23.990 --> 00:47:26.600
in the saddlebags, who
weighs only a few pounds

00:47:26.600 --> 00:47:29.300
and never talks back,
not to turn your bicycle

00:47:29.300 --> 00:47:33.050
into a motorcycle, but to let
you do for 30 minutes what

00:47:33.050 --> 00:47:36.590
your normal ability would let
you do for only three minutes.

00:47:36.590 --> 00:47:38.150
You can extend your
commuting range

00:47:38.150 --> 00:47:41.820
with his bionic hybrid that
will never run out of gas.

00:47:41.820 --> 00:47:44.280
You can match performance
between individuals

00:47:44.280 --> 00:47:48.660
or not be bothered by
hills on a hot afternoon.

00:47:48.660 --> 00:47:51.360
A tiny airplane, the
AV Pointer, serves

00:47:51.360 --> 00:47:53.430
for surveillance,
in effect a pair

00:47:53.430 --> 00:47:56.880
of roving eyeglasses, a
cutting edge example of where

00:47:56.880 --> 00:47:59.220
miniaturization can
lead if the operator is

00:47:59.220 --> 00:48:00.990
remote from the vehicle.

00:48:00.990 --> 00:48:04.560
It is convenient to carry,
assemble, and launch by hand.

00:48:04.560 --> 00:48:08.070
Battery powered, it is
silent and rarely noticed.

00:48:08.070 --> 00:48:11.700
It sends high-resolution video
pictures back to the operator.

00:48:11.700 --> 00:48:15.480
With onboard GPS, it can
navigate autonomously.

00:48:15.480 --> 00:48:20.370
And it is rugged enough to
self-land without damage.

00:48:20.370 --> 00:48:23.230
The modern sailplane
is superbly efficient.

00:48:23.230 --> 00:48:25.380
Some can glide as
flat as 60 feet

00:48:25.380 --> 00:48:27.990
forward for every
foot of descent.

00:48:27.990 --> 00:48:29.820
They are powered
only by the energy

00:48:29.820 --> 00:48:32.320
they can extract
from the atmosphere,

00:48:32.320 --> 00:48:35.460
an atmosphere Nature
stirs up by solar energy.

00:48:35.460 --> 00:48:37.650
Humans and soaring
birds have found

00:48:37.650 --> 00:48:41.220
Nature can be generous in
providing replenishable energy.

00:48:41.220 --> 00:48:44.070
Sailplanes have flown
over 1,000 miles,

00:48:44.070 --> 00:48:51.230
and the altitude record
is over 50,000 feet.

00:48:51.230 --> 00:48:54.290
The Solar Challenger was
made to serve as a symbol

00:48:54.290 --> 00:48:57.530
that photovoltaic cells
can produce real power

00:48:57.530 --> 00:49:00.110
and will be part of the
world's energy future.

00:49:00.110 --> 00:49:04.790
In 1981, it flew 163 miles
from Paris to England, solely

00:49:04.790 --> 00:49:08.160
on the power of sunbeams,
and established a basis

00:49:08.160 --> 00:49:09.270
for the Pathfinder.

00:49:12.180 --> 00:49:14.260
The message from
all these vehicles

00:49:14.260 --> 00:49:16.810
is that ideas and
technology can be harnessed

00:49:16.810 --> 00:49:21.580
to produce remarkable gains
in doing more with less, gains

00:49:21.580 --> 00:49:24.160
that can help us attain
a desirable balance

00:49:24.160 --> 00:49:26.260
between technology and Nature.

00:49:26.260 --> 00:49:30.940
The stakes are high as we speed
toward a challenging future.

00:49:30.940 --> 00:49:33.310
Buckminster Fuller
said it clearly.

00:49:33.310 --> 00:49:37.570
"There are no passengers on
Spaceship Earth, only crew."

00:49:37.570 --> 00:49:43.315
We, the crew, can and must
do more with less, much less.

00:49:43.315 --> 00:49:44.260
[END PLAYBACK]

00:49:44.260 --> 00:49:45.642
MACCREADY: Thank you very much.

00:49:45.642 --> 00:49:48.594
[APPLAUSE]

00:49:57.460 --> 00:49:58.760
John Langford takes over.

00:50:09.540 --> 00:50:11.040
LANGFORD: Thank you
very much, Paul.

00:50:11.040 --> 00:50:12.915
And I'd like to say
thank you very much, too,

00:50:12.915 --> 00:50:14.640
to Ed Crawley for
organizing this.

00:50:14.640 --> 00:50:18.090
This is a great opportunity
and a great event

00:50:18.090 --> 00:50:20.593
to put two programs
together here.

00:50:20.593 --> 00:50:23.010
What I'd like to do, if we
could bring the first slide up,

00:50:23.010 --> 00:50:26.130
bring the lights down
and the first slide up,

00:50:26.130 --> 00:50:31.530
is to take up where Paul
left off, in many ways,

00:50:31.530 --> 00:50:33.960
in the story of
human-powered flight,

00:50:33.960 --> 00:50:37.690
and talk for a few minutes
about the MIT side of it.

00:50:37.690 --> 00:50:40.770
And in particular, it's the
Daedalus Project, obviously,

00:50:40.770 --> 00:50:43.230
that has epitomized this.

00:50:43.230 --> 00:50:45.900
But I'd like to speak as
well about the whole history

00:50:45.900 --> 00:50:49.170
of this, with an emphasis
on the role of these things

00:50:49.170 --> 00:50:51.720
as student teaching
tools, because they

00:50:51.720 --> 00:50:55.440
have played a unique role in
the department over the last 20

00:50:55.440 --> 00:50:57.090
years or so.

00:50:57.090 --> 00:51:01.260
This is the famous
image of Daedalus 88

00:51:01.260 --> 00:51:05.190
arriving at Santorini
10 years ago

00:51:05.190 --> 00:51:07.920
just last week, after
about a four-hour

00:51:07.920 --> 00:51:12.960
flight over 72 miles, 115
kilometers, the product

00:51:12.960 --> 00:51:16.650
of a three-year program
here at MIT and piloted

00:51:16.650 --> 00:51:18.090
by Kanellos Kanellopoulos.

00:51:18.090 --> 00:51:21.495
And it's a very great pleasure
to have Kanellos here today.

00:51:21.495 --> 00:51:23.370
If you could stand up
for a minute, Kanellos.

00:51:23.370 --> 00:51:26.550
This is his first
time in the United--

00:51:26.550 --> 00:51:29.430
[APPLAUSE]

00:51:34.040 --> 00:51:35.630
This is the first
time that Kanellos

00:51:35.630 --> 00:51:37.400
has returned to MIT
since he was here

00:51:37.400 --> 00:51:39.200
before the flight training.

00:51:39.200 --> 00:51:42.980
And we're delighted
to have him and so

00:51:42.980 --> 00:51:45.980
many other members of
the Daedalus team back.

00:51:45.980 --> 00:51:49.070
So I mentioned
the project really

00:51:49.070 --> 00:51:51.260
goes back 20 years or so.

00:51:51.260 --> 00:51:54.620
And many of the themes that Paul
was talking about a few minutes

00:51:54.620 --> 00:51:56.960
ago play right in in here.

00:51:56.960 --> 00:52:00.920
The Kremer prize served as the
impetus for and the stimulus

00:52:00.920 --> 00:52:03.620
for a number of student
projects beginning here

00:52:03.620 --> 00:52:05.660
in the early 1970s.

00:52:05.660 --> 00:52:08.780
This aircraft was one of two
called the Biplane Ultralight

00:52:08.780 --> 00:52:11.300
Research Device, or BURD,
which were built here

00:52:11.300 --> 00:52:14.870
in the Department by the
two teams or several teams

00:52:14.870 --> 00:52:17.840
of students during
the early 1970s.

00:52:17.840 --> 00:52:21.320
This was what the finished
configuration looked like.

00:52:21.320 --> 00:52:27.710
This was about a 60-foot
span, two-person aircraft.

00:52:27.710 --> 00:52:32.705
And as Paul has just narrated
the story of the Gossamer

00:52:32.705 --> 00:52:37.310
Condor, once the Condor won
the Kremer Prize, the first one

00:52:37.310 --> 00:52:40.370
in 1977 for the
figure-of-eight flight,

00:52:40.370 --> 00:52:45.260
interest in these projects
languished somewhat.

00:52:45.260 --> 00:52:48.620
As Paul mentioned, there was
a subsequent prize offered

00:52:48.620 --> 00:52:50.570
for cross-channel flight.

00:52:50.570 --> 00:52:53.420
There had been a number of
other activities here at MIT.

00:52:53.420 --> 00:52:57.770
The one that our group
particularly came out of

00:52:57.770 --> 00:53:01.850
was the model aircraft group
and the Model Rocket Society,

00:53:01.850 --> 00:53:04.340
which had been actually
pioneering the techniques

00:53:04.340 --> 00:53:07.040
that Paul showed a few
minutes ago in that micro air

00:53:07.040 --> 00:53:08.990
vehicle flying
around inside, which

00:53:08.990 --> 00:53:13.430
is now a program supported by
DARPA, the Defense Projects

00:53:13.430 --> 00:53:14.360
Agency.

00:53:14.360 --> 00:53:17.990
And one of the big advances
here at MIT in the late 1970s

00:53:17.990 --> 00:53:21.020
was the miniaturization of
radio control technology

00:53:21.020 --> 00:53:24.200
and its application to these
small, radio-controlled boost

00:53:24.200 --> 00:53:28.070
gliders, here flying for the US
team in the World Championships

00:53:28.070 --> 00:53:33.860
in 1978, where Harold Guppy
Youngren won the first World

00:53:33.860 --> 00:53:36.050
Championship in this event.

00:53:38.750 --> 00:53:42.920
We got involved in the activity
of human-powered aircraft.

00:53:42.920 --> 00:53:44.960
Here you can see
there's Guppy, the world

00:53:44.960 --> 00:53:49.160
champion on the right, and
Professor Drela in the center,

00:53:49.160 --> 00:53:54.590
driven by the interest in this
as student projects supposed

00:53:54.590 --> 00:53:58.060
to be fun and interesting.

00:53:58.060 --> 00:54:00.500
As Paul alluded
to, though, there

00:54:00.500 --> 00:54:02.700
were some very serious
aspects of this, as well.

00:54:02.700 --> 00:54:06.860
And one of the first areas
that the MIT group really

00:54:06.860 --> 00:54:08.390
was able to
distinguish itself in

00:54:08.390 --> 00:54:10.400
was the design and
the application

00:54:10.400 --> 00:54:13.580
of numerical methods to
airfoils, and particularly

00:54:13.580 --> 00:54:14.330
to propellers.

00:54:14.330 --> 00:54:17.660
And as Paul mentioned,
Gene Larrabee

00:54:17.660 --> 00:54:21.260
was influential in this,
bringing the methods of Pranel

00:54:21.260 --> 00:54:23.330
and Betz and Gloward
and updating them

00:54:23.330 --> 00:54:25.170
from for computer execution.

00:54:25.170 --> 00:54:27.770
And it was really a generation
of students there working

00:54:27.770 --> 00:54:30.740
under Gene that coded that up.

00:54:30.740 --> 00:54:34.070
And the propeller became
the first real visible

00:54:34.070 --> 00:54:35.860
implementation of this.

00:54:35.860 --> 00:54:40.310
This is Mark hot-wiring a
piece of that propeller,

00:54:40.310 --> 00:54:43.370
these large, four-meter
diameter props built

00:54:43.370 --> 00:54:47.310
in little six-inch-long
sections here.

00:54:47.310 --> 00:54:50.420
And then, obviously, there
was interchange back and forth

00:54:50.420 --> 00:54:54.770
all throughout this
with the two teams.

00:54:54.770 --> 00:55:00.500
We were able to design the
propeller for the Gossamer

00:55:00.500 --> 00:55:01.580
Albatross aircraft.

00:55:01.580 --> 00:55:04.940
And Paul contributed
enormously to the efforts

00:55:04.940 --> 00:55:08.450
here at MIT, providing
a lot of tips

00:55:08.450 --> 00:55:10.640
about how these
aircraft went together,

00:55:10.640 --> 00:55:12.470
particularly the
lessons from the Condor

00:55:12.470 --> 00:55:15.470
and the Albatross
experience, and as

00:55:15.470 --> 00:55:16.770
well as providing material.

00:55:16.770 --> 00:55:22.400
All of the skin covering, the
Mylar for the first Chrysalis

00:55:22.400 --> 00:55:25.310
aircraft that we built
were donated by Paul

00:55:25.310 --> 00:55:26.150
as part of this.

00:55:26.150 --> 00:55:28.970
This is the spring of 1979.

00:55:28.970 --> 00:55:31.940
Guppy and Paul here,
and I think this

00:55:31.940 --> 00:55:33.770
is in Henry Jex's backyard.

00:55:33.770 --> 00:55:37.832
Henry is here today
from Santa Monica.

00:55:37.832 --> 00:55:39.290
This was the aircraft
that resulted

00:55:39.290 --> 00:55:42.170
from our efforts,
Chrysalis, a large biplane,

00:55:42.170 --> 00:55:46.420
72-foot wingspan, not
really intended at the time

00:55:46.420 --> 00:55:49.280
to be a serious contender
for the cross-channel prize,

00:55:49.280 --> 00:55:53.420
but as a way of getting a new
generation of MIT students

00:55:53.420 --> 00:55:54.860
into the air.

00:55:54.860 --> 00:55:58.640
It was distinguished, I
think, by a number of firsts.

00:55:58.640 --> 00:56:02.660
Certainly at this point, it
had among the highest number

00:56:02.660 --> 00:56:04.970
of flights ever made,
something like 350 flights

00:56:04.970 --> 00:56:08.690
on this airframe without a
crash or serious rebuild on it,

00:56:08.690 --> 00:56:11.680
and holds at least one record
that continues to stand,

00:56:11.680 --> 00:56:13.430
which is to our
knowledge, the only person

00:56:13.430 --> 00:56:16.460
who has ever flown both
under their own power

00:56:16.460 --> 00:56:21.890
and has flown in space is
Jay Apt, another MIT graduate

00:56:21.890 --> 00:56:23.880
who flew the Chrysalis
among many others

00:56:23.880 --> 00:56:27.430
in the summer of 1979 there.

00:56:27.430 --> 00:56:30.710
The Chrysalis project
was overcome, of course,

00:56:30.710 --> 00:56:34.710
by the Albatross, which won
the Kremer Cross-channel

00:56:34.710 --> 00:56:37.950
Prize in June of '79, a few
days after Chrysalis made

00:56:37.950 --> 00:56:43.530
its first flight, which I think
Paul has narrated fabulously

00:56:43.530 --> 00:56:44.830
for us there.

00:56:44.830 --> 00:56:47.850
And there was a subsequent
series of Kremer competitions

00:56:47.850 --> 00:56:50.640
called the Speed Prizes
to make airplanes smaller

00:56:50.640 --> 00:56:52.650
and hopefully, more practical.

00:56:52.650 --> 00:56:56.070
We were able to win first
place in that competition

00:56:56.070 --> 00:57:00.210
with an aircraft here
called The Monarch, which

00:57:00.210 --> 00:57:06.420
was built and flown in 1983
and 1984 out at Hanscom Field

00:57:06.420 --> 00:57:07.380
there.

00:57:07.380 --> 00:57:10.260
The idea here was to fly
a sort of a metric mile

00:57:10.260 --> 00:57:11.640
flight, 1,500 meters.

00:57:11.640 --> 00:57:14.910
And the first one in under
three minutes, I think,

00:57:14.910 --> 00:57:20.280
won the 30,000 pound
prize for that flight.

00:57:20.280 --> 00:57:22.830
That was a great experience.

00:57:22.830 --> 00:57:26.810
But the combination of
the Chrysalis experience,

00:57:26.810 --> 00:57:30.450
the Monarch experience
and the Gossamer series

00:57:30.450 --> 00:57:32.760
left a number of
us wondering where

00:57:32.760 --> 00:57:35.220
this would lead if
it wasn't driven

00:57:35.220 --> 00:57:38.340
by the impetus of the prizes.

00:57:38.340 --> 00:57:40.800
The prizes certainly
provided a good focus point,

00:57:40.800 --> 00:57:43.440
but they also put an
emphasis on how fast

00:57:43.440 --> 00:57:45.820
and how quick and dirty
could you do this.

00:57:45.820 --> 00:57:48.090
And there was an
element to this of,

00:57:48.090 --> 00:57:50.460
if you didn't have
that as a stimulus,

00:57:50.460 --> 00:57:53.778
and you were just trying
to push it to the max,

00:57:53.778 --> 00:57:55.320
how far could one
of these things go?

00:57:55.320 --> 00:57:57.607
How far had the technology
really progressed?

00:57:57.607 --> 00:57:59.940
Because you have to remember,
it was moving very quickly

00:57:59.940 --> 00:58:02.430
in this period, with
computational aerodynamics,

00:58:02.430 --> 00:58:06.120
composite structures coming in.

00:58:06.120 --> 00:58:07.380
How far could you push this?

00:58:07.380 --> 00:58:09.060
And that led us
into the thinking

00:58:09.060 --> 00:58:13.350
of was it possible to take this
all the way back to the oldest

00:58:13.350 --> 00:58:17.190
Western reference of man flying
under his own initiative-- that

00:58:17.190 --> 00:58:19.050
is, the story of
Daedalus and Icarus,

00:58:19.050 --> 00:58:22.470
which was the team,
according to legend,

00:58:22.470 --> 00:58:25.560
imprisoned on the
island of Crete

00:58:25.560 --> 00:58:29.100
back in the Late Bronze Age, who
built wings of feathers and wax

00:58:29.100 --> 00:58:33.560
and flew to freedom.

00:58:33.560 --> 00:58:38.220
Two aircraft built there,
obviously, a story most famous

00:58:38.220 --> 00:58:41.580
because of the two,
the son, Icarus,

00:58:41.580 --> 00:58:43.720
flew in the story
too close to the Sun,

00:58:43.720 --> 00:58:45.720
which melted the wax
holding the wings together.

00:58:45.720 --> 00:58:47.600
And he fell to his death.

00:58:47.600 --> 00:58:50.580
As is true even to
today, the crashes

00:58:50.580 --> 00:58:52.050
get all of the attention.

00:58:52.050 --> 00:58:54.630
And history had pretty
much neglected the idea

00:58:54.630 --> 00:58:58.800
that his father, Daedalus,
had made it safely.

00:58:58.800 --> 00:59:02.580
And that story became
the driving force

00:59:02.580 --> 00:59:06.210
behind the idea of
the Daedalus Project

00:59:06.210 --> 00:59:08.918
here at MIT in the middle 1980s.

00:59:08.918 --> 00:59:09.960
This is the flight route.

00:59:09.960 --> 00:59:11.910
When we first started
on that project,

00:59:11.910 --> 00:59:13.698
we assumed rather
naively that it

00:59:13.698 --> 00:59:15.240
would be possible,
as good engineers,

00:59:15.240 --> 00:59:17.640
to go look all this up, and
find the exact flight route,

00:59:17.640 --> 00:59:21.343
and literally recreate the
route that Daedalus had flown.

00:59:21.343 --> 00:59:23.010
Obviously, as we began
to get into that,

00:59:23.010 --> 00:59:25.950
we learned a lot
about a lot of things

00:59:25.950 --> 00:59:28.157
during this project,
classical mythology

00:59:28.157 --> 00:59:29.740
being one of the
first, and of course,

00:59:29.740 --> 00:59:33.420
that there is no literal
recreation possible--

00:59:33.420 --> 00:59:36.030
that myth-making is
a creative process

00:59:36.030 --> 00:59:37.290
and builds up over time.

00:59:37.290 --> 00:59:41.040
But applying the navigational
strategies of the day

00:59:41.040 --> 00:59:45.330
as mariners practiced them
to the idea of flight,

00:59:45.330 --> 00:59:46.830
it was pretty clear
that anybody who

00:59:46.830 --> 00:59:50.063
was trying to fly from
Crete and Knossos--

00:59:50.063 --> 00:59:51.480
the ruins were
actually discovered

00:59:51.480 --> 00:59:53.070
in the early part
of the century here

00:59:53.070 --> 00:59:54.900
on the north part of Crete--

00:59:54.900 --> 00:59:58.350
the nearest major landmass is up
here, the island of Santorini,

00:59:58.350 --> 01:00:01.950
which is 72 miles
or 115 kilometers,

01:00:01.950 --> 01:00:04.920
about three times the distance
of the cross-channel flight,

01:00:04.920 --> 01:00:06.180
north of the island.

01:00:06.180 --> 01:00:08.790
And whatever your
eventual destination was--

01:00:08.790 --> 01:00:13.518
and the myths do disagree on
the eventual destination--

01:00:13.518 --> 01:00:15.810
the first step would have
been to get off to the island

01:00:15.810 --> 01:00:18.660
and to get to Santorini.

01:00:18.660 --> 01:00:23.080
And so that became the flight
route that we undertook.

01:00:23.080 --> 01:00:25.120
So we began looking into this.

01:00:25.120 --> 01:00:26.550
It was a research
program not only

01:00:26.550 --> 01:00:29.130
in aeronautics, as I mentioned,
but in many other areas.

01:00:29.130 --> 01:00:31.020
The meteorology was
an important one,

01:00:31.020 --> 01:00:34.140
as we began to look into
the weather in the region.

01:00:34.140 --> 01:00:37.110
Obviously, Paul talked a
little about the difficulties

01:00:37.110 --> 01:00:39.480
that Bryan faced both in
terms of the physiology

01:00:39.480 --> 01:00:41.040
and the meteorology.

01:00:41.040 --> 01:00:43.770
We began to address
those right off the bat.

01:00:43.770 --> 01:00:45.510
The existing weather
database didn't

01:00:45.510 --> 01:00:47.670
have the resolution down
in the low-wind conditions

01:00:47.670 --> 01:00:48.330
that we needed.

01:00:48.330 --> 01:00:50.400
So one of the first
steps was to deploy

01:00:50.400 --> 01:00:52.410
a series of small,
automated weather stations

01:00:52.410 --> 01:00:54.720
there for a year or
so before the flight,

01:00:54.720 --> 01:00:58.500
as we looked for an envelope
of weather conditions--

01:00:58.500 --> 01:01:01.890
calm wind for four to six
hours and low temperatures.

01:01:01.890 --> 01:01:05.010
Temperatures were very
important because of the engine.

01:01:05.010 --> 01:01:07.380
An engine research program
was undertaken down

01:01:07.380 --> 01:01:09.900
at Yale University.

01:01:09.900 --> 01:01:13.950
This is Dr. Ethan Edl, who was
the project's lead physiologist

01:01:13.950 --> 01:01:17.640
in a program that first
attempted to sort of quantify

01:01:17.640 --> 01:01:19.548
what human performance
really was,

01:01:19.548 --> 01:01:21.090
so that we could
use it as engineers,

01:01:21.090 --> 01:01:23.150
and put it into the
aircraft sizing,

01:01:23.150 --> 01:01:26.365
and optimization models.

01:01:26.365 --> 01:01:27.990
In addition to the
physiology, probably

01:01:27.990 --> 01:01:30.720
the single most important
technical advance

01:01:30.720 --> 01:01:34.100
was numerical aerodynamics,
computational aerodynamics

01:01:34.100 --> 01:01:37.130
and as practiced by Mark Drela.

01:01:37.130 --> 01:01:40.275
I think those of you who
attended the symposium here,

01:01:40.275 --> 01:01:41.150
it's really striking.

01:01:41.150 --> 01:01:43.660
One of the real things that
came out of this program--

01:01:43.660 --> 01:01:44.510
Mark's codes.

01:01:44.510 --> 01:01:45.410
Now, of course
that may have been

01:01:45.410 --> 01:01:47.600
a criterion for getting
invited to this symposium.

01:01:47.600 --> 01:01:50.450
But I think that
every single speaker

01:01:50.450 --> 01:01:53.210
there, on whichever
coast or continent,

01:01:53.210 --> 01:01:57.530
is using a version of the
ISES or MCs or XROTOR codes

01:01:57.530 --> 01:02:01.370
that Mark developed, and that
were first implemented here

01:02:01.370 --> 01:02:03.980
in the Daedalus
project, which provided

01:02:03.980 --> 01:02:07.160
the aerodynamic designs
on all of the flight

01:02:07.160 --> 01:02:10.900
surfaces-- propeller,
wing, tails, everything.

01:02:10.900 --> 01:02:14.810
Another big part of
this was the structures.

01:02:14.810 --> 01:02:17.360
To get the kind of
performance we were needing,

01:02:17.360 --> 01:02:20.420
we really had to maintain
large area of laminar flow.

01:02:20.420 --> 01:02:23.630
That was partly computationally
done through Mark's codes,

01:02:23.630 --> 01:02:25.870
but then we had
to implement that.

01:02:25.870 --> 01:02:28.490
The structures had to be built
that were capable of sustaining

01:02:28.490 --> 01:02:29.780
that laminar flow.

01:02:29.780 --> 01:02:31.790
And numerically
controlled foam cutter

01:02:31.790 --> 01:02:35.870
was designed and built, and
used to precisely cut out

01:02:35.870 --> 01:02:38.810
all of the different
airfoil shapes

01:02:38.810 --> 01:02:40.880
that went into covering
the leading edge.

01:02:40.880 --> 01:02:42.422
You can kind of look
at the Daedalus,

01:02:42.422 --> 01:02:46.520
and see that the pink areas
are the areas of laminar flow.

01:02:46.520 --> 01:02:49.880
Obviously, the foam
isn't very strong,

01:02:49.880 --> 01:02:52.100
and you have to have
a backbone to that,

01:02:52.100 --> 01:02:55.580
which was the job
of Juan Cruz, who

01:02:55.580 --> 01:02:58.520
is now down at NASA Langley
as their composites expert.

01:02:58.520 --> 01:03:04.970
But this was an all-composite
aircraft with one wire bracing.

01:03:04.970 --> 01:03:07.910
And this is a picture
of the full wing

01:03:07.910 --> 01:03:12.740
being proof-loaded in a test
out at the Lincoln Labs hangar.

01:03:15.430 --> 01:03:18.440
I wanted to recognize
the critical role played

01:03:18.440 --> 01:03:20.750
by Jack Kerrebrock in
all of these projects,

01:03:20.750 --> 01:03:24.200
because Jack has been
the project's advisor

01:03:24.200 --> 01:03:25.070
and sponsor in this.

01:03:25.070 --> 01:03:28.100
Every time we started
one of these projects,

01:03:28.100 --> 01:03:29.892
once we got past the
initial brainstorming,

01:03:29.892 --> 01:03:31.767
the first thing we had
to do was go into Jack

01:03:31.767 --> 01:03:32.750
and ask for some money.

01:03:32.750 --> 01:03:35.980
And whether it was
in his office here

01:03:35.980 --> 01:03:37.820
as Department
Chair for Chrysalis

01:03:37.820 --> 01:03:40.160
or when he was at Monarch,
we had to go down to--

01:03:40.160 --> 01:03:41.870
he was a NASA Associate
Administrator--

01:03:41.870 --> 01:03:42.980
we had to go down to NASA.

01:03:42.980 --> 01:03:44.798
And when he was
back for Daedalus,

01:03:44.798 --> 01:03:46.340
he had moved up to
the Dean's office.

01:03:46.340 --> 01:03:49.970
And Jack has been a
critical supporter of this

01:03:49.970 --> 01:03:51.290
throughout the whole history.

01:03:51.290 --> 01:03:53.207
And none of this would
have happened, I think,

01:03:53.207 --> 01:03:55.300
without his support.

01:03:55.300 --> 01:03:57.200
We got the support
from unusual places.

01:03:57.200 --> 01:03:58.850
One of the ones was--

01:03:58.850 --> 01:04:01.280
the main sponsor for the
prototype of the Daedalus

01:04:01.280 --> 01:04:03.020
was Anheuser-Busch.

01:04:03.020 --> 01:04:07.910
And the aircraft was named by
them, the Michelob Light Eagle.

01:04:07.910 --> 01:04:11.000
And obviously, their target
market was the college crowd.

01:04:11.000 --> 01:04:16.340
Here's Tom Clancy modeling
for an Anheuser-Busch ad,

01:04:16.340 --> 01:04:18.240
I think, in this one.

01:04:18.240 --> 01:04:22.670
And that's the aircraft
flying in January of 1987

01:04:22.670 --> 01:04:26.020
out over Rogers Dry
Lake in California.

01:04:26.020 --> 01:04:29.330
It was flown for a series
of five record flights

01:04:29.330 --> 01:04:32.660
by Lois McCallin
and Glenn Tremml,

01:04:32.660 --> 01:04:37.740
including records over straight
line and closed course records.

01:04:37.740 --> 01:04:41.840
It broke the Albatross's
record with a 37-mile flight.

01:04:41.840 --> 01:04:44.390
And then the Light Eagle went
in and served as a test bed.

01:04:44.390 --> 01:04:45.920
It was heavily instrumented.

01:04:45.920 --> 01:04:48.398
You can see the
different instrumentation

01:04:48.398 --> 01:04:49.190
that was put on it.

01:04:49.190 --> 01:04:51.920
It became a real flight
research aircraft,

01:04:51.920 --> 01:04:54.290
collecting the
quantitative data that

01:04:54.290 --> 01:04:57.860
was needed both for
the Daedalus airplane

01:04:57.860 --> 01:05:00.140
itself, for subsequent
human-powered airplanes,

01:05:00.140 --> 01:05:02.120
and also for some of
the high-altitude things

01:05:02.120 --> 01:05:06.020
that Paul alluded to, the very
lightweight, very flexible

01:05:06.020 --> 01:05:08.900
structures that you need
in solar-powered aircraft,

01:05:08.900 --> 01:05:09.830
for example.

01:05:09.830 --> 01:05:10.730
This was a test bed.

01:05:10.730 --> 01:05:14.127
NASA sponsored part of
their research program.

01:05:14.127 --> 01:05:15.710
And there was a lot
of instrumentation

01:05:15.710 --> 01:05:17.210
that went on the airplane.

01:05:17.210 --> 01:05:19.490
Of course, all of you
associated with the project

01:05:19.490 --> 01:05:21.860
know that the title
of this picture is--

01:05:21.860 --> 01:05:22.850
"I don't understand.

01:05:22.850 --> 01:05:24.608
It worked back in Cambridge."

01:05:27.890 --> 01:05:31.070
By the time the Light Eagle
had completed its program,

01:05:31.070 --> 01:05:34.220
we moved into the
Daedalus phase itself.

01:05:34.220 --> 01:05:36.860
And we began the construction
of two very refined

01:05:36.860 --> 01:05:39.680
versions of the Light Eagle,
which were the actual Daedalus

01:05:39.680 --> 01:05:40.540
planes.

01:05:40.540 --> 01:05:42.830
And at this point, we
began to transition

01:05:42.830 --> 01:05:44.630
to yet another
generation of students.

01:05:44.630 --> 01:05:47.300
The UROP program, the
Undergraduate Research

01:05:47.300 --> 01:05:49.960
Opportunities Program,
played a big role in this.

01:05:49.960 --> 01:05:53.480
This is Guppy here, who
served as the Chief Engineer

01:05:53.480 --> 01:05:56.180
on the Daedalus
planes, instructing

01:05:56.180 --> 01:06:00.260
Tom Clancy in some fine point
of how these aircraft are built.

01:06:00.260 --> 01:06:02.180
This is Juan with
two other UROPs

01:06:02.180 --> 01:06:04.730
here in a composite
test program,

01:06:04.730 --> 01:06:09.290
as we began every piece of
the structure in the Daedalus

01:06:09.290 --> 01:06:11.780
planes underwent
proof load testing.

01:06:11.780 --> 01:06:16.220
And then the numbers were fed
back into the final designs.

01:06:16.220 --> 01:06:20.510
And most of the construction
was done by the UROPs.

01:06:20.510 --> 01:06:23.165
This was, at times, a
controversial strategy.

01:06:23.165 --> 01:06:25.040
There were some members
of the department who

01:06:25.040 --> 01:06:28.790
grumbled that the project
was using the UROP

01:06:28.790 --> 01:06:30.050
program for slave labor.

01:06:30.050 --> 01:06:33.050
And it was never clear to me
where they got that impression,

01:06:33.050 --> 01:06:36.590
but anyway.

01:06:36.590 --> 01:06:39.530
And we did work hard to employ
everybody in the program.

01:06:39.530 --> 01:06:41.810
The pilots, even when
they couldn't help build,

01:06:41.810 --> 01:06:42.980
we put them to work.

01:06:42.980 --> 01:06:46.130
Here, they are selling patches
onto the team jackets the night

01:06:46.130 --> 01:06:49.320
before the rollout.

01:06:49.320 --> 01:06:56.130
This was the Daedalus plane,
as finished in late 1987.

01:06:56.130 --> 01:06:58.133
The rollout was in October.

01:06:58.133 --> 01:06:59.550
I always remember
that, because it

01:06:59.550 --> 01:07:02.580
was the day of the great
1987 stock market crash,

01:07:02.580 --> 01:07:05.460
and much to the dismay
of the people from United

01:07:05.460 --> 01:07:08.100
Technologies, because there
was no mention of the Daedalus

01:07:08.100 --> 01:07:12.270
rollout because of
the large crash.

01:07:12.270 --> 01:07:14.260
There were other crashes
later in the program.

01:07:14.260 --> 01:07:19.050
This was one that occurred
in January, I guess actually

01:07:19.050 --> 01:07:21.010
early February of 1988.

01:07:21.010 --> 01:07:25.080
This was about 21 days prior
to the shipment to Greece.

01:07:25.080 --> 01:07:27.900
We had a Greek C-130
scheduled to come

01:07:27.900 --> 01:07:30.420
and pick the airplane up.

01:07:30.420 --> 01:07:33.090
And we had four
minutes of flight time.

01:07:33.090 --> 01:07:36.420
And then we had
the airplane crash.

01:07:36.420 --> 01:07:40.620
We lost the plane,
the first plane in--

01:07:40.620 --> 01:07:45.000
that was February of 1988.

01:07:45.000 --> 01:07:47.070
Fortunately, we had been
working on a backup.

01:07:47.070 --> 01:07:50.460
And we brought the
wreckage of the first plane

01:07:50.460 --> 01:07:54.570
back, rushed the completion of
the second airplane forward.

01:07:54.570 --> 01:08:00.810
And by early March of '88, we
had the second plane flying.

01:08:00.810 --> 01:08:06.930
And the cause of the accident
was essentially typical,

01:08:06.930 --> 01:08:09.580
not uncommon in
engineering projects.

01:08:09.580 --> 01:08:11.910
It's a failure to
communicate among two

01:08:11.910 --> 01:08:14.160
parts of the engineering
team and the design team.

01:08:14.160 --> 01:08:16.939
And the fundamental problem
was that the aircraft, which

01:08:16.939 --> 01:08:20.750
had depended on its dihedral
for roll-yaw coupling,

01:08:20.750 --> 01:08:24.779
for lateral control,
the bracing wire which

01:08:24.779 --> 01:08:28.140
really controls that dihedral
angle was cut too short.

01:08:28.140 --> 01:08:30.779
And that wasn't caught in
the manufacturing or checkout

01:08:30.779 --> 01:08:31.350
process.

01:08:31.350 --> 01:08:33.779
And the aircraft didn't
have adequate control.

01:08:33.779 --> 01:08:37.420
Once we had traced that
problem and analyzed it,

01:08:37.420 --> 01:08:38.460
it was very easy to fix.

01:08:38.460 --> 01:08:40.649
And the second aircraft
flew beautifully,

01:08:40.649 --> 01:08:43.560
made an abbreviated
series of test flights,

01:08:43.560 --> 01:08:49.300
and then was packed up in a
C-130 and sent over to Greece.

01:08:49.300 --> 01:08:51.450
Once in Greece, there
was a lot going on there.

01:08:51.450 --> 01:08:54.180
Obviously, the Aegean
is a region noted

01:08:54.180 --> 01:08:55.890
for its changeable weather.

01:08:55.890 --> 01:08:57.870
And the aircraft
had to be protected,

01:08:57.870 --> 01:09:01.319
so the UROPs went to work
again, and we built a hangar.

01:09:01.319 --> 01:09:05.430
We had purchased a prefabricated
hangar and taken it over.

01:09:05.430 --> 01:09:07.979
That team went over a
few days ahead of time.

01:09:07.979 --> 01:09:10.890
We had secured a small fleet
of inflatable boats, which

01:09:10.890 --> 01:09:14.229
had been loaned to us by several
organizations over there.

01:09:14.229 --> 01:09:18.359
The engines had been provided
here in the United States.

01:09:18.359 --> 01:09:22.290
And so we converted the
UROP team from builders

01:09:22.290 --> 01:09:23.098
into boat teams.

01:09:23.098 --> 01:09:25.140
The first couple of drills
here were pretty wild,

01:09:25.140 --> 01:09:28.290
with people overboard, and
radios that went in the water,

01:09:28.290 --> 01:09:31.359
and all of that that went
into making the flight.

01:09:31.359 --> 01:09:33.960
But we had plenty of
time to sort this out,

01:09:33.960 --> 01:09:36.694
because as the meteorology
got worked through--

01:09:36.694 --> 01:09:38.069
and this is Steve
Bussolari here,

01:09:38.069 --> 01:09:40.680
the Director of Flight
Operations on the program--

01:09:40.680 --> 01:09:42.643
we didn't get very
many opportunities.

01:09:42.643 --> 01:09:44.310
We knew that we
wouldn't have very many.

01:09:44.310 --> 01:09:48.029
That we had five or six a
year was the prediction.

01:09:48.029 --> 01:09:50.228
And we were up against
a weather cut-off,

01:09:50.228 --> 01:09:52.020
because it would get
too hot in the summer.

01:09:52.020 --> 01:09:54.210
We simply didn't
have the capability

01:09:54.210 --> 01:09:56.190
to keep flying into
the summer because

01:09:56.190 --> 01:09:59.670
of the high temperatures that we
would encounter in the region.

01:09:59.670 --> 01:10:02.660
We did have several
false alarms,

01:10:02.660 --> 01:10:06.360
where we got up and went through
the whole flight deployment

01:10:06.360 --> 01:10:09.210
drill, had the pilot
sealed up in the cockpit

01:10:09.210 --> 01:10:12.030
there, sitting on the
runway ready to go.

01:10:12.030 --> 01:10:16.340
This is Mark, and I think that's
Frank Scioscia in the cockpit

01:10:16.340 --> 01:10:18.275
on this, ready to go.

01:10:18.275 --> 01:10:19.650
And then at the
very last minute,

01:10:19.650 --> 01:10:21.600
making the call that
the conditions were

01:10:21.600 --> 01:10:24.450
not what we wanted
all along the route,

01:10:24.450 --> 01:10:28.530
and scrubbing the flight,
and putting the whole thing

01:10:28.530 --> 01:10:30.410
back in.

01:10:30.410 --> 01:10:32.780
There was a lot of waiting.

01:10:32.780 --> 01:10:34.790
The plane was
ready and there was

01:10:34.790 --> 01:10:38.550
a lot of waiting, which
was a very anxious period.

01:10:38.550 --> 01:10:41.870
It's a beautiful
area there, but it

01:10:41.870 --> 01:10:46.250
was hard to relax and
enjoy it waiting for this.

01:10:46.250 --> 01:10:48.090
There was a lot of
other dynamics going on.

01:10:48.090 --> 01:10:50.090
I love this picture,
because it kind of captures

01:10:50.090 --> 01:10:52.850
all of the players we
were dealing with in here.

01:10:52.850 --> 01:10:55.070
The military got involved
because, of course,

01:10:55.070 --> 01:10:57.543
we were operating off
a Greek military base.

01:10:57.543 --> 01:10:58.460
That's pretty obvious.

01:10:58.460 --> 01:11:00.110
The Church was a
little less obvious.

01:11:00.110 --> 01:11:03.140
The Church was involved
because the issue came up

01:11:03.140 --> 01:11:05.720
of what if the weather was
perfect on Easter Sunday?

01:11:05.720 --> 01:11:07.820
And there was great
debate within the team

01:11:07.820 --> 01:11:09.300
about how to handle that.

01:11:09.300 --> 01:11:10.820
We finally went
to the archbishop,

01:11:10.820 --> 01:11:14.330
and briefed him on
the project, and asked

01:11:14.330 --> 01:11:15.380
what his guidance was.

01:11:15.380 --> 01:11:16.820
He said, go for it.

01:11:16.820 --> 01:11:19.740
He said, if the weather's
good, you guys go for it.

01:11:22.250 --> 01:11:23.917
Gasoline turned out
to be another factor

01:11:23.917 --> 01:11:25.875
that you wouldn't have
thought would be a limit

01:11:25.875 --> 01:11:27.110
in a human-powered aircraft.

01:11:27.110 --> 01:11:29.040
Obviously, the airplane
didn't need it.

01:11:29.040 --> 01:11:30.170
But all the boats did.

01:11:30.170 --> 01:11:33.610
And this was the
supplies that we

01:11:33.610 --> 01:11:39.280
needed for one day of flying,
or the boat support for a day

01:11:39.280 --> 01:11:42.040
of flight operations there.

01:11:42.040 --> 01:11:44.480
The people who kept the busiest,
I think, were the pilots.

01:11:44.480 --> 01:11:47.750
We had a team of five
of them on rotation.

01:11:47.750 --> 01:11:50.840
This was because we
had figured that this

01:11:50.840 --> 01:11:54.830
was going to be right
at the limit of what

01:11:54.830 --> 01:11:57.560
human physiology was
capable of, and we

01:11:57.560 --> 01:12:00.770
had recruited and trained
five world-class athletes who

01:12:00.770 --> 01:12:03.980
were in a rotation, much
like baseball pitchers, where

01:12:03.980 --> 01:12:08.630
they were on for two days
and then in a rotation cycle

01:12:08.630 --> 01:12:10.950
off for four, six,
or eight days,

01:12:10.950 --> 01:12:12.560
depending on how
many were healthy.

01:12:12.560 --> 01:12:17.130
Obviously, injuries were
another big part of this.

01:12:17.130 --> 01:12:19.970
So the training regime
was very important.

01:12:19.970 --> 01:12:26.690
They were riding hundreds
of miles each day to train.

01:12:26.690 --> 01:12:29.450
They obviously came into the
project as excellent athletes.

01:12:29.450 --> 01:12:31.340
They were all
Olympic-level cyclists

01:12:31.340 --> 01:12:33.505
when they joined the project.

01:12:33.505 --> 01:12:34.880
And in the course
of the project,

01:12:34.880 --> 01:12:36.963
they were all trained to
be highly skilled pilots.

01:12:36.963 --> 01:12:39.230
A lot of that was in
high-performance sailplanes.

01:12:39.230 --> 01:12:43.130
They started in,
obviously, lower training

01:12:43.130 --> 01:12:44.690
sailplanes, and
quickly moved into

01:12:44.690 --> 01:12:46.290
high-performance sailplanes.

01:12:46.290 --> 01:12:48.290
A lot of the training
took place on a simulator,

01:12:48.290 --> 01:12:51.500
which combined a visual
out the window display

01:12:51.500 --> 01:12:52.880
with an ergometer.

01:12:52.880 --> 01:12:55.340
Your power was actually being
measured here as you pedaled.

01:12:55.340 --> 01:12:59.360
And that power reading was
fed into the simulation

01:12:59.360 --> 01:13:00.990
and displayed.

01:13:00.990 --> 01:13:05.900
So you had the full flight
controls, as well as the power

01:13:05.900 --> 01:13:07.263
going into the simulation.

01:13:07.263 --> 01:13:08.930
And then once all of
that was completed,

01:13:08.930 --> 01:13:10.160
you would fly the Light Eagle.

01:13:10.160 --> 01:13:12.202
The Light Eagle had become
the training airplane.

01:13:12.202 --> 01:13:14.210
And once you had gone
through this regime,

01:13:14.210 --> 01:13:15.050
you flew the Eagle.

01:13:15.050 --> 01:13:16.508
And one of the
requirements was you

01:13:16.508 --> 01:13:20.472
had to get through a minimum
of a two-hour long flight,

01:13:20.472 --> 01:13:22.430
a number of flights in
the Eagle, including one

01:13:22.430 --> 01:13:24.800
at least two hours
long, before you

01:13:24.800 --> 01:13:27.980
could get into the Daedalus.

01:13:27.980 --> 01:13:30.920
And once you had gone
through all of that,

01:13:30.920 --> 01:13:33.800
you were checked out
to fly the Daedalus.

01:13:33.800 --> 01:13:37.380
This is one pilot's
food ration for one day.

01:13:37.380 --> 01:13:41.300
And it's pretty amazing.

01:13:41.300 --> 01:13:44.000
The nutritionists from
Shaklee Corporation

01:13:44.000 --> 01:13:46.760
who were a sponsor at this time
helped go over, and sort of

01:13:46.760 --> 01:13:49.260
rewrote the whole
diet of the kitchen

01:13:49.260 --> 01:13:51.320
in the place we were staying.

01:13:51.320 --> 01:13:53.683
And the pilots had this.

01:13:53.683 --> 01:13:55.100
At first, all the
rest of the team

01:13:55.100 --> 01:13:56.898
was pretty envious
of this, because this

01:13:56.898 --> 01:13:57.690
looked pretty good.

01:13:57.690 --> 01:14:00.438
But then it turned
out, as the day wore on

01:14:00.438 --> 01:14:01.980
and they realized
that these guys had

01:14:01.980 --> 01:14:04.130
to eat the same
thing every day, it

01:14:04.130 --> 01:14:05.770
became a little less attractive.

01:14:05.770 --> 01:14:10.240
The rest of the team got to vary
their meal a little bit more.

01:14:10.240 --> 01:14:13.610
The in-flight meal service
was provided by Ethan Edl.

01:14:13.610 --> 01:14:15.800
This is "Ethan-ol",
as we called it.

01:14:15.800 --> 01:14:18.140
It was a very
high-performance drink

01:14:18.140 --> 01:14:21.140
which had been optimized to
be right at the limits of what

01:14:21.140 --> 01:14:23.060
a human could tolerate
in the system,

01:14:23.060 --> 01:14:28.130
in terms of replenishing
the glycogen that you needed

01:14:28.130 --> 01:14:32.210
to keep your
muscles functioning.

01:14:32.210 --> 01:14:36.270
And Ethan had moved into this,
and had developed this drink,

01:14:36.270 --> 01:14:40.970
had debugged it on the pilots
through various versions.

01:14:40.970 --> 01:14:44.960
And there's actually a
toned-down version, a consumer

01:14:44.960 --> 01:14:49.210
version, I think, that
Shaklee is still marketing.

01:14:49.210 --> 01:14:50.892
There were, what
was it, six quarts

01:14:50.892 --> 01:14:52.850
of that on the airplane
or something like that,

01:14:52.850 --> 01:14:55.690
six liters, four liters.

01:14:55.690 --> 01:14:58.810
After a period of what seemed
like interminable waiting--

01:14:58.810 --> 01:15:01.390
it was probably more like
three weeks now in retrospect--

01:15:01.390 --> 01:15:03.730
but we finally did
get a day where

01:15:03.730 --> 01:15:07.800
things began to really line
up across the whole Aegean.

01:15:07.800 --> 01:15:13.480
And the crew was brought out
at, I guess 2:00 AM wake-up.

01:15:13.480 --> 01:15:17.230
And assembly of the
aircraft began at about 4:00

01:15:17.230 --> 01:15:18.190
in the morning.

01:15:18.190 --> 01:15:23.620
And the plane was put
together there on the runway.

01:15:23.620 --> 01:15:25.720
A lot of white
knuckles in the boats,

01:15:25.720 --> 01:15:27.310
and watching as we got ready.

01:15:27.310 --> 01:15:32.710
And then Kanellos was given
the go-ahead and took off.

01:15:32.710 --> 01:15:36.700
I mentioned that Kanellos was
the pilot, by luck of the draw

01:15:36.700 --> 01:15:40.360
here that his number
came up on the day

01:15:40.360 --> 01:15:42.820
that the weather
conditions were good.

01:15:42.820 --> 01:15:45.850
And so shortly after
7:00 AM, Kanellos

01:15:45.850 --> 01:15:49.150
took off from this
runway, and went

01:15:49.150 --> 01:15:53.030
over the edge of this
embankment here, and headed out.

01:15:53.030 --> 01:15:55.920
And the airplane which
had looked so huge when

01:15:55.920 --> 01:15:58.840
you were up close to it suddenly
looked completely different

01:15:58.840 --> 01:16:01.450
in terms of something
that was tiny

01:16:01.450 --> 01:16:04.215
as he set out on this slide.

01:16:04.215 --> 01:16:06.340
You can see in the background
there the big ferries

01:16:06.340 --> 01:16:09.760
that go every night
to and from Athens.

01:16:09.760 --> 01:16:12.490
And you can see the inflatables
that are accompanying it,

01:16:12.490 --> 01:16:15.730
in case the aircraft
had to ditch.

01:16:15.730 --> 01:16:18.190
We were prepared to take
the airplane under tow.

01:16:18.190 --> 01:16:20.590
We had tried to absorb
all of the lessons

01:16:20.590 --> 01:16:22.390
from the Albatross project.

01:16:22.390 --> 01:16:25.480
And Albatross's
strategy, for example,

01:16:25.480 --> 01:16:27.350
was to take a
fishing line and try

01:16:27.350 --> 01:16:29.170
to hook a ring on the aircraft.

01:16:29.170 --> 01:16:31.480
Daedalus had a
refined system that

01:16:31.480 --> 01:16:33.468
had a tow line in the
airplane, so in the event

01:16:33.468 --> 01:16:35.260
that Kanellos wanted
to be taken under two,

01:16:35.260 --> 01:16:38.080
he would have dropped
the line, and the boat

01:16:38.080 --> 01:16:40.330
underneath taken it
up, and towed it.

01:16:40.330 --> 01:16:43.210
Navigation was done
from a command boat

01:16:43.210 --> 01:16:47.490
following behind
the aircraft there.

01:16:47.490 --> 01:16:48.860
This is a nice shot looking up.

01:16:48.860 --> 01:16:51.520
You can see the carbon
structure there,

01:16:51.520 --> 01:16:54.700
and the region of
laminar flow on the top.

01:16:54.700 --> 01:16:57.490
It really is laminar
back to about 60%.

01:16:57.490 --> 01:17:00.700
Pretty much you can see
where the foam goes to

01:17:00.700 --> 01:17:02.235
on the top surface.

01:17:02.235 --> 01:17:03.610
The bottom is
almost all laminar.

01:17:03.610 --> 01:17:04.800
It didn't need the foam.

01:17:04.800 --> 01:17:06.050
And here you get another view.

01:17:06.050 --> 01:17:09.070
This is looking straight
down on the aircraft,

01:17:09.070 --> 01:17:12.250
and again, a good view
of the laminar flow.

01:17:14.980 --> 01:17:19.120
We had escorts provided by the
Greek Navy and the Greek Coast

01:17:19.120 --> 01:17:19.690
Guard.

01:17:19.690 --> 01:17:23.500
Their primary role
was in case we

01:17:23.500 --> 01:17:26.590
needed any emergency
medical rescue.

01:17:26.590 --> 01:17:29.440
That was provided by the boats.

01:17:29.440 --> 01:17:32.080
And they also helped
keep the route

01:17:32.080 --> 01:17:36.100
clear from other boats that
would have gotten in the way.

01:17:36.100 --> 01:17:39.280
This is a great picture,
gives you a sense of the speed

01:17:39.280 --> 01:17:43.000
at which Kanellos
was flying, and also

01:17:43.000 --> 01:17:45.010
the crosswinds that
he was managing.

01:17:45.010 --> 01:17:47.400
You can see the crab in there.

01:17:47.400 --> 01:17:49.210
The boats, obviously,
and the wakes

01:17:49.210 --> 01:17:55.530
are marking the direct flight
route to Santorini there.

01:17:55.530 --> 01:17:57.230
And this picture
gives you a good sense

01:17:57.230 --> 01:18:01.850
of the smooth surface of
the upper part of the wing.

01:18:01.850 --> 01:18:05.030
This is another good example
of the "doing more with less"

01:18:05.030 --> 01:18:06.410
that Paul was just
talking about,

01:18:06.410 --> 01:18:09.320
where the speedboat there has--

01:18:09.320 --> 01:18:11.900
what is that, 120
horse motor on there,

01:18:11.900 --> 01:18:16.490
130 horse motor on the back
of that speedboat there.

01:18:16.490 --> 01:18:19.940
The Daedalus has a quarter
horsepower motor in it,

01:18:19.940 --> 01:18:21.560
and they're going
the same speed.

01:18:21.560 --> 01:18:25.610
And it's all due to the
refinement of the technology

01:18:25.610 --> 01:18:29.180
and the application of
technology to energy efficiency

01:18:29.180 --> 01:18:31.550
here.

01:18:31.550 --> 01:18:36.140
The flight had been projected
to take as long as six hours.

01:18:36.140 --> 01:18:39.440
We had trained for six hours,
but the conditions were good,

01:18:39.440 --> 01:18:42.830
and Kanellos was just fantastic.

01:18:42.830 --> 01:18:45.980
His heart rate when he
started was about 156,

01:18:45.980 --> 01:18:47.733
and declined
throughout the flight.

01:18:47.733 --> 01:18:49.400
By the time we were
inside of Santorini,

01:18:49.400 --> 01:18:54.560
his heart rate was down to
126, which still just boggles

01:18:54.560 --> 01:18:59.073
my mind, how cool he was
throughout all of this.

01:18:59.073 --> 01:19:00.740
Actually, by this
time, most of the team

01:19:00.740 --> 01:19:02.390
had deployed to
the landing site,

01:19:02.390 --> 01:19:09.800
and was watching, marking
the site there with smoke.

01:19:09.800 --> 01:19:11.750
And then Kanellos had
the choice of going

01:19:11.750 --> 01:19:13.190
straight in onto the beach.

01:19:13.190 --> 01:19:17.160
A lot has been made of the fact
that the aircraft broke up.

01:19:17.160 --> 01:19:19.160
I'm here to say, we were
maneuvering this thing

01:19:19.160 --> 01:19:20.673
to try to get it
back in one piece.

01:19:20.673 --> 01:19:23.090
Kanellos easily could have
gone straight in onto the beach

01:19:23.090 --> 01:19:26.660
if the objective was just
to get it onto the beach,

01:19:26.660 --> 01:19:29.090
but we really wanted
to try to maneuver

01:19:29.090 --> 01:19:30.690
the airplane in and land it.

01:19:30.690 --> 01:19:34.130
We had all seen that sight
of Bryan Allen slipping it

01:19:34.130 --> 01:19:35.780
in there onto the
beach in France.

01:19:35.780 --> 01:19:37.610
And that was pretty
much the maneuver

01:19:37.610 --> 01:19:38.610
we were working on here.

01:19:38.610 --> 01:19:40.940
So Kanellos came in
at fairly high speed,

01:19:40.940 --> 01:19:43.250
turns in parallel to
the beach, and tries

01:19:43.250 --> 01:19:46.130
to slip it in parallel.

01:19:46.130 --> 01:19:49.235
Here, you can see the
beginning of the failure.

01:19:49.235 --> 01:19:51.110
You can actually see
the buckling right there

01:19:51.110 --> 01:19:55.910
in the carbon tail boom, as a
gust of wind, thermally induced

01:19:55.910 --> 01:19:59.120
coming off this black sand,
catches this, torques the tail.

01:19:59.120 --> 01:20:00.470
Tail fails right there.

01:20:00.470 --> 01:20:02.180
The tail boom fails.

01:20:02.180 --> 01:20:05.030
The wires or the cables
that control the elevator

01:20:05.030 --> 01:20:06.150
are outside at that point.

01:20:06.150 --> 01:20:09.740
So as this torques, it
puts a full-up command

01:20:09.740 --> 01:20:12.890
on the horizontal stab there.

01:20:12.890 --> 01:20:14.522
And that overstresses the wing.

01:20:14.522 --> 01:20:16.730
You can see it fails out
right near a transport joint

01:20:16.730 --> 01:20:18.290
on the right-hand side.

01:20:18.290 --> 01:20:24.950
And the aircraft drops into the
water there just off the shore.

01:20:24.950 --> 01:20:27.940
We had a lot of people help us
take it apart at this point.

01:20:27.940 --> 01:20:30.000
That was no problem.

01:20:30.000 --> 01:20:31.820
If you go to Santorini,
I'm sure there's

01:20:31.820 --> 01:20:35.870
still pink foam souvenirs
in some of the shops there.

01:20:35.870 --> 01:20:40.790
And there's Kanellos after
the flight, who no kidding,

01:20:40.790 --> 01:20:46.460
could have gone on for quite
a while on this flight.

01:20:46.460 --> 01:20:49.955
And that's the team there
on the beach, many of whom

01:20:49.955 --> 01:20:50.580
are here today.

01:20:50.580 --> 01:20:52.288
Could I get everybody
who was on the team

01:20:52.288 --> 01:20:53.960
to stand up at this
point, everybody

01:20:53.960 --> 01:20:55.460
who worked on the project?

01:20:55.460 --> 01:20:57.052
Could you stand.

01:20:57.052 --> 01:21:00.028
[APPLAUSE]

01:21:13.647 --> 01:21:15.730
Many of the people I think
haven't seen each other

01:21:15.730 --> 01:21:17.490
in 10 years after this.

01:21:17.490 --> 01:21:20.480
And so I really do
thank Ed Crawley.

01:21:20.480 --> 01:21:22.610
For pulling this
whole thing together.

01:21:22.610 --> 01:21:25.890
The Daedalus project
was done for education.

01:21:25.890 --> 01:21:27.170
It was done for research.

01:21:27.170 --> 01:21:28.310
It was a marvelous project.

01:21:28.310 --> 01:21:31.370
It could never answer
the question of, so what,

01:21:31.370 --> 01:21:33.230
when people asked
it in the lectures.

01:21:33.230 --> 01:21:37.363
But one of the interesting
things about science

01:21:37.363 --> 01:21:38.780
when you really
are doing research

01:21:38.780 --> 01:21:42.830
is that you never know
where the next problem is

01:21:42.830 --> 01:21:44.090
going to come from.

01:21:44.090 --> 01:21:45.770
And in Daedalus,
for me, anyway, it

01:21:45.770 --> 01:21:47.860
came from a very
unexpected source.

01:21:47.860 --> 01:21:50.240
In the mid '80s,
there were two things

01:21:50.240 --> 01:21:52.850
that emerged that kind
of shifted the paradigm

01:21:52.850 --> 01:21:56.450
from nuclear holocaust being
the sort of big-picture issue

01:21:56.450 --> 01:22:00.410
that kind of hung
over all of us.

01:22:00.410 --> 01:22:02.480
And those two issues were
the emergence of AIDS,

01:22:02.480 --> 01:22:04.980
the breakout of that into
the general population,

01:22:04.980 --> 01:22:06.890
and the depletion of
stratospheric ozone,

01:22:06.890 --> 01:22:10.100
in terms of issues that when
they first began to appear,

01:22:10.100 --> 01:22:12.470
no one was quite sure how
bad this was going to be,

01:22:12.470 --> 01:22:13.970
and that clearly,
had the potential

01:22:13.970 --> 01:22:22.140
to eliminate life as we knew
it in a worst-case scenario.

01:22:22.140 --> 01:22:25.430
The ozone hole, obviously,
appeared over Antarctica

01:22:25.430 --> 01:22:26.660
in the early '80s.

01:22:26.660 --> 01:22:28.910
And the mid '80s,
a lot of attention

01:22:28.910 --> 01:22:32.180
was directed towards figuring
out exactly what it was.

01:22:32.180 --> 01:22:34.100
The primary tools
for doing that were

01:22:34.100 --> 01:22:37.580
large stratospheric
balloons and an aircraft

01:22:37.580 --> 01:22:39.860
called the ER-2,
which NASA operates.

01:22:39.860 --> 01:22:42.350
It's a version of the
military spy plane.

01:22:42.350 --> 01:22:44.930
And these worked
okay for figuring out

01:22:44.930 --> 01:22:48.320
the causes of the
Antarctic ozone hole,

01:22:48.320 --> 01:22:50.540
and providing the
conclusive links

01:22:50.540 --> 01:22:54.410
between chlorofluorocarbons
and the catalytic cycles

01:22:54.410 --> 01:22:55.760
that destroyed the ozone.

01:22:55.760 --> 01:22:57.650
But it was also clear
that the scientists,

01:22:57.650 --> 01:22:59.067
to answer the
question of, is this

01:22:59.067 --> 01:23:01.010
going to spread to other
places on the planet,

01:23:01.010 --> 01:23:03.560
is this going to happen in
the northern hemisphere,

01:23:03.560 --> 01:23:05.990
is it going to happen
over the middle latitudes,

01:23:05.990 --> 01:23:08.420
that you needed to go
out and take measurements

01:23:08.420 --> 01:23:11.420
at higher altitudes and in
more places around the planet.

01:23:11.420 --> 01:23:15.260
And a couple of us from
the Daedalus project

01:23:15.260 --> 01:23:18.140
linked up with a group down
at Harvard in the Atmospheric

01:23:18.140 --> 01:23:20.030
Research project
there, and began

01:23:20.030 --> 01:23:21.830
construction of a
series of aircraft

01:23:21.830 --> 01:23:24.740
that would attempt to
apply the aerodynamics

01:23:24.740 --> 01:23:28.070
and the structures, and some
of the project management

01:23:28.070 --> 01:23:30.140
philosophies that
came out of Daedalus,

01:23:30.140 --> 01:23:34.220
and apply them to a
series of small, very

01:23:34.220 --> 01:23:36.290
customized, robotic
aircraft that

01:23:36.290 --> 01:23:38.030
could be used for
atmospheric science,

01:23:38.030 --> 01:23:41.360
high-altitude research,
initially in the ozone area.

01:23:41.360 --> 01:23:45.740
And we built an aircraft
called Perseus, about 60,

01:23:45.740 --> 01:23:49.820
62 feet in wingspan,
developed the controls

01:23:49.820 --> 01:23:52.910
technology for that, and
made a series of flights.

01:23:52.910 --> 01:23:57.860
This is really to give you a
sense of scale of the Perseus.

01:23:57.860 --> 01:24:00.050
This particular
aircraft has carried

01:24:00.050 --> 01:24:05.600
about a 220-pound payload to an
altitude of about 50,000 feet.

01:24:05.600 --> 01:24:09.650
And both the solar plane
and the Perseus aircraft

01:24:09.650 --> 01:24:13.820
are working together, are joint
participants in a NASA program

01:24:13.820 --> 01:24:16.940
that's developing the technology
for future operational

01:24:16.940 --> 01:24:18.170
aircraft.

01:24:18.170 --> 01:24:22.640
And we've also applied that to
a larger aircraft that's really

01:24:22.640 --> 01:24:26.390
designed specifically around the
needs of the scientists, called

01:24:26.390 --> 01:24:26.960
Theseus.

01:24:26.960 --> 01:24:29.330
And that's the larger,
twin-engine aircraft

01:24:29.330 --> 01:24:29.960
in the back.

01:24:29.960 --> 01:24:35.210
And we have built-- this is a
140-foot wingspan aircraft that

01:24:35.210 --> 01:24:42.170
carries between 750 and 1,500
pounds of instruments onboard,

01:24:42.170 --> 01:24:44.960
and eventually to
altitudes of 85,000 feet

01:24:44.960 --> 01:24:47.360
or so for fairly
long endurances.

01:24:47.360 --> 01:24:50.030
This is an airplane
that's in development,

01:24:50.030 --> 01:24:56.060
and is a direct outgrowth of
the Daedalus technologies.

01:24:56.060 --> 01:24:58.280
I wanted to close here.

01:24:58.280 --> 01:25:02.050
And if I have the time
here, which I think I do,

01:25:02.050 --> 01:25:07.960
Corey can you queue
up this short video?

01:25:07.960 --> 01:25:10.640
As you put that in, I just want
to close with a few thoughts

01:25:10.640 --> 01:25:17.540
here on this picture, which
is the layered structure

01:25:17.540 --> 01:25:23.540
of the atmosphere here, which
is really a neat picture showing

01:25:23.540 --> 01:25:25.830
the whole path here.

01:25:25.830 --> 01:25:28.760
And I think the lessons that
Paul has been talking about,

01:25:28.760 --> 01:25:31.130
certainly the lessons that
we have drawn from Daedalus,

01:25:31.130 --> 01:25:35.150
support very much the idea
that the atmosphere is,

01:25:35.150 --> 01:25:38.150
although it looks very large,
it looks like it's an infinite

01:25:38.150 --> 01:25:40.280
sink for many purposes,
it really isn't.

01:25:40.280 --> 01:25:44.180
If you look at this
picture, above that line

01:25:44.180 --> 01:25:48.200
is space, a very
hostile environment.

01:25:48.200 --> 01:25:52.880
Below that line is Earth and
everything on it that we know

01:25:52.880 --> 01:25:53.900
and hold dear.

01:25:53.900 --> 01:25:56.510
And that atmosphere
is a very thin line

01:25:56.510 --> 01:25:59.300
that separates those
two from each other.

01:25:59.300 --> 01:26:00.860
It's amazingly under-sampled.

01:26:00.860 --> 01:26:02.910
There's still a lot to be done.

01:26:02.910 --> 01:26:08.570
And to the future generations of
students who are coming along,

01:26:08.570 --> 01:26:10.670
hopefully some of whom
are in the room attending

01:26:10.670 --> 01:26:13.590
this lecture today, there
is still a lot to be done.

01:26:13.590 --> 01:26:15.590
And these types of programs
that Paul and I have

01:26:15.590 --> 01:26:19.660
talked about here are
great ways to get into it.

01:26:19.660 --> 01:26:22.200
I'd like you, if you
can cue up this video,

01:26:22.200 --> 01:26:26.090
this is a piece that Brian
Sullivan edited together

01:26:26.090 --> 01:26:29.526
of the flight.

01:26:29.526 --> 01:26:32.520
[VIDEO PLAYBACK]

01:32:07.848 --> 01:32:08.540
[END PLAYBACK]

01:32:08.540 --> 01:32:11.016
Okay, thank you very, very much.

01:32:11.016 --> 01:32:14.502
[APPLAUSE]

01:32:20.990 --> 01:32:22.900
MODERATOR: Well, I
think we have a chance

01:32:22.900 --> 01:32:25.302
to entertain a few
questions from the audience,

01:32:25.302 --> 01:32:26.260
and then we'll wrap up.

01:32:31.749 --> 01:32:34.743
AUDIENCE: I'd like to hear
the tale of the [INAUDIBLE]..

01:32:37.738 --> 01:32:40.280
MODERATOR: Why don't-- Kanellos,
why don't you come down here

01:32:40.280 --> 01:32:40.988
and tell us that?

01:32:40.988 --> 01:32:42.440
And then we'll close on that.

01:32:58.520 --> 01:33:01.205
KANELLOPOULOS: First, thank
you for the opportunity

01:33:01.205 --> 01:33:03.030
you give me to talk.

01:33:03.030 --> 01:33:07.250
Sorry for my bad English.

01:33:07.250 --> 01:33:13.680
About the last
moment, there when

01:33:13.680 --> 01:33:18.240
I was pedaling towards
Santorini was a tailwind.

01:33:18.240 --> 01:33:21.495
So no problem, no big problems.

01:33:21.495 --> 01:33:27.990
And over there, as John said,
because of the high speed

01:33:27.990 --> 01:33:35.250
we had, they decide
to make me do a turn.

01:33:35.250 --> 01:33:36.720
So we did the turn.

01:33:36.720 --> 01:33:39.690
But during the turn,
we faced the wind.

01:33:39.690 --> 01:33:44.920
And the wind approaching
the land was too hard.

01:33:44.920 --> 01:33:49.050
So the plane was going up and
down, like you have seen there.

01:33:49.050 --> 01:33:51.780
This plane was not designed
to do all those things.

01:33:55.060 --> 01:33:58.910
So it resisted too much.

01:33:58.910 --> 01:34:03.320
And one moment, I felt--
no, it was not my feeling.

01:34:03.320 --> 01:34:04.520
It was real.

01:34:04.520 --> 01:34:06.230
The plane was
flying, but staying

01:34:06.230 --> 01:34:09.020
in the same place,
because that wind had

01:34:09.020 --> 01:34:11.280
the speed of the plane.

01:34:11.280 --> 01:34:14.090
So it was very tough.

01:34:14.090 --> 01:34:20.600
And then, it took a
bank, and it was not

01:34:20.600 --> 01:34:25.280
possible to make it return back.

01:34:25.280 --> 01:34:30.170
So it hit it once, hit it
twice, and then broken the tail.

01:34:30.170 --> 01:34:33.500
So it was-- but it was closed.

01:34:33.500 --> 01:34:38.150
But I think this end, because
it was not so easy and smooth

01:34:38.150 --> 01:34:42.800
like the other, make a very--

01:34:42.800 --> 01:34:46.290
put fear to someone else
who wants to do the same,

01:34:46.290 --> 01:34:49.027
that it's not so easy.

01:34:49.027 --> 01:34:52.021
[APPLAUSE]

01:34:58.652 --> 01:35:00.110
MODERATOR: Well,
with that, I think

01:35:00.110 --> 01:35:02.600
we'll conclude this
year's Gardner Lecture.

01:35:02.600 --> 01:35:03.940
Thank you very much for coming.

01:35:03.940 --> 01:35:06.990
[APPLAUSE]

